\section{Conclusion}
% In conclusion, our proposed data-driven weight initialization technique, ConsNoTrainLoRA (CNTLoRA), demonstrates significant improvements in both convergence speed and final performance of low-rank Adapters (LoRA) in fine-tuning tasks. By framing the LoRA initialization as a domain shift problem and leveraging constraints on activation vectors, we derived a closed-form estimate for the LoRA weights. This approach effectively bridges the gap between pre-training and fine-tuning phases, leading to enhanced performance across various downstream tasks such as image generation, image classification, image understanding. Our extensive analyses and ablations further validate the robustness and efficacy of CNTLoRA, providing valuable insights into optimal design choices for parameter-efficient fine-tuning. The common insight is that our cross mode ie. CNTLoRA-X produces the highest performance most of the case. Furthermore, we allowed flexibility of rank budget through a variable adapter structure which showed improved recognition performance on the image generation task.
% Future work will explore additional constraints and their impact on other PEFT methods, aiming to further refine and expand the applicability of our framework.

In conclusion, our data-driven weight initialization technique, CNTLoRA, significantly improves convergence speed and final performance of LoRA in fine-tuning tasks. By treating LoRA initialization as a domain shift problem and using activation vector constraints, we derived a closed-form estimate for LoRA weights. This enhances performance across tasks like image generation, classification, and understanding. Our analyses validate CNTLoRA’s robustness and efficacy, with CNTLoRA-X achieving better performance in few shot adaptation of the image generation task, while CNTLoRA-S producing better performance in the many-shot adaptation of image classification and understanding. Additionally, our proposed variable adapter structure improved recognition performance by allowing optimal rank allocation. Future work will explore more constraints to refine and expand our framework’s applicability.

