On the 536 commonly solved instances, both the sequential and the parallel approach made a total of 6181 SAT solving calls.
5645 calls resulted in unsatisfiability (``UNSAT calls'') and the remaining 536 in satisfiability (``SAT calls'').
Fig.~\ref{fig:mallotane-satcall-cdfs} shows how the duration of these calls is distributed for the sequential and the parallel backend.
With the sequential backend, 96.86\% of UNSAT calls and 57.65\% of SAT calls took less than one second---confirming that the performance of our planner crucially depends on low overhead for individual calls.
The break-off point where our \mallob{} backend begins to outperform the sequential backend is at 0.23\,s:
The same ratio of calls (90.4\%) finished within this time for both approaches.
For UNSAT calls this break-off point is at 0.28\,s (95.1\% of calls finished) while for SAT calls it is already at 0.053\,s (31.7\% of calls finished).
