\title[DHoTT: Temporal HoTT]{DHoTT: A Temporal Extension of Homotopy Type Theory for Semantic Drift}

\author[I. Poernomo]{Iman Poernomo}
\address{Data and Analytics, London Stock Exchange Group}
\email{iman.poernomo@lseg.com}


\keywords{Type theory, semantic drift, univalence, rupture types, temporal logic}




\begin{abstract}
We introduce \emph{Dynamic Homotopy Type Theory (DHoTT)}, a temporal \linebreak extension of Homotopy Type Theory (HoTT) designed to reason formally about concepts whose meanings evolve continuously or rupture discontinuously over context-time. \linebreak Traditional HoTT captures identity and equivalence within a fixed semantic landscape; DHoTT enriches this structure by explicitly indexing types by a context-time parameter $\tau$, allowing types themselves to deform, rupture, and reassemble over time.

Formally, we show that DHoTT is the internal language of $[(\mathbb{R}, \leq)^{\mathrm{op}}, \mathbf{SSet}]$. \linebreak Consequently, our type theory:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Conservatively extends HoTT, recovering standard Homotopy Type Theory whenever context-time is frozen;
  \item Preserves all standard HoTT structures including univalence and higher inductive types;
  \item Introduces new syntactic constructs--\emph{drift paths} and \emph{rupture types}--for precisely reasoning about semantic evolution and discontinuous conceptual shifts.
\end{itemize}

We illustrate the utility of DHoTT with a running example derived from \linebreak conversational dynamics in contemporary large language models, highlighting its \linebreak expressiveness and applicability to research in posthuman intelligence and evolving meaning.
\end{abstract}

