Several studies have already demonstrated that effective communication (i.e., the exchange of information) among nodes significantly influences the performance of the network, including convergence speed and the quality of collaboration \cite{olfati2007consensus}.
As mentioned in the previous paragraph, communication methods (i.e., periodic or aperiodic) also play a vital role in the network performance.
%the number of events when the data transmission and energy usage constraints.
Furthermore, the number of neighboring nodes (i.e., connectivity) as well as the weights of the communication links (i.e., edge weights) affect the communication in the network \cite{krivzmanvcic2024adaptive}.
%Furthermore, the number of neighboring nodes (i.e., connectivity) in the network affects the communication in the network along with the weights of the communication links (i.e., edge weights) \cite{krivzmanvcic2024adaptive}.
To this end, we seek an answer to the following scientific question: %\textit{Can we utilize time-varying edge weights as a control tool in such a way that it improves the performance of the multiagent network while reducing (or not increasing) the number of events?}
