\section{INCOMPATIBILITY ISSUES}
As mentioned previously, we encountered a few issues due to the incompatibility between the default software and devices. Therefore, this section showed our approaches using coding and programming commands to resolve incompatibility issues between the Autoware platform and the AV's interface. The solution codes and error messages are provided during the installation in the study for interested users to follow for troubleshooting purposes. 

\subsection{Python version management}
Ubuntu 20.04 is compatible with both Python 2 and 3. Python 2 is the default interpreter when executing the \texttt{Python –V} command. To change the system's default Python interpreter setting, execute commands in the Terminal under the ROS-based Ubuntu environment, such as \texttt{sudo ln -s /usr/bin/python3 /usr/bin/python}. 

\paragraph{"python-catkin-pkg" has no installation } Try several commands: 
\begin{verbatim}
sudo apt install -y python3-catkin-pkg python3-rosdep ros-$ROS_DISTRO-catkin
sudo apt install -y python3-pip python3-colcon-common-extensions python3-setuptools
python3-vcstool
pip3 install -U setuptools
\end{verbatim}

\paragraph{No module named "wx"} Install wxPython using commands as follows; either works.
\begin{verbatim}
python3 -m pip install -U -f https://extras.wxpython.org/wxPython4/extras/linux/gtk3/
ubuntu-20.04 wxPython 
sudo pip3 install wxpython.
\end{verbatim}

\paragraph{wxPython verification} \texttt{python3 -m pip}


\subsection{System dependencies (e.g., CUDA)}
\paragraph{Checking CUDA version}
First, check the CUDA version using the \texttt{nvcc –version} command. If no information is shown in the Terminal, install CUDA by referring to NVIDIA's official web regarding the GPU product. We solved this issue using the following commands \cite{cuda}.
\begin{enumerate}[topsep=0pt,itemsep=-1ex,partopsep=1ex,parsep=1ex]
\item Add repository for CUDA 11: \texttt{wget \url{https://developer.download.nvidia.com/compute/cuda/repos/ubuntu2004/x86_64/cuda-ubuntu2004.pin}}

\item Add public key: 
\begin{verbatim}
    sudo mv cuda-ubuntu2004.pin /etc/apt/preferences.d/cuda-repository-pin-600
    sudo add-apt-repository "deb http://developer.download.nvidia.com/compute/cuda
    /repos/ubuntu2004/x86_64/ /"
\end{verbatim}

\item Upgrade the installed packages and check the CUDA version.
\texttt{sudo apt update} and \texttt{sudo apt install nvdia-cuda-toolkit-VERSION}
\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Autoware.AI installation}
Autoware.AI was directly downloaded from the Git repository of the AV contractor's contractor, and the software was customized after the repositories were cloned from the contractor.
\paragraph{Creating project directory}
We created a main directory, 'autoware.ai,' for Autoware.ai, and then a subdirectory, "src," for the source files using the following commands: 

\texttt{mkdir -p autoware.ai/src}

\texttt{cd autoware.ai/src}

\paragraph{Cloning the repositories from the contractor}
There are five steps to install Autoware.AI. We created a workspace in the PC and downloaded the workspace configuration for Autoware using the following commands:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/common.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/core\_perception.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/core\_planning.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/docker.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/drivers.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/messages.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/simulation.git}
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/utilities.git} 
  \item \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/astuff/autoware.ai/visualization.git} 
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Check branch}
There could be several versions of the Git branch. We need to check out the "oap9" branch first. After cloning the repositories, we navigated through each directory and checked out the desired branch ("oap9") using the \texttt{git checkout branch\_name} command. The following shell commands can also be used to check out all the repositories. 
\begin{verbatim}
#!/bin/bash
for dir in autoware common core_perception \
    core_planning docker drivers messages simulation \
    utilities visualization; do
  if [ -d "$dir/.git" ]; then
    cd "$dir" && git checkout oap9)
  else
    echo "Skipping $dir, not a Git repository."
  fi
done
\end{verbatim}

\paragraph{Compile the Workspace}
After checking the branch, we continued the process and compiled the Autoware.AI with or without CUDA support \cite{autostuff}. If desired, the following command can be utilized to compile Autoware.AI with CUDA support:
\begin{verbatim}
AUTOWARE_COMPILE_WITH_CUDA=1 colcon build --cmake-args -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Compile customized Autoware.AI}
We noticed that the Git repository of Autoware.AI shared by the contractor does not have simulation features \cite{autorepo}, and parts of their codebases are still dependent on Python 2. As our ADS platform is configured for Python 3, we encountered several issues requiring manual intervention and corrections. So, we ended up manually performing the debugging process and eventually fixed the issues.

After addressing the incompatibility issues within Autoware.ai, we created a private GitLab repository to manage our modified version, with plans to make it public. Since Autoware.AI contains over a hundred packages, fixing and compiling these packages is time-consuming and prone to errors. To streamline this process, we uploaded the compiled version of Autoware.AI to our GitLab repository using the following command without recompilation \cite{ruman}: \texttt{git clone https://gitlab.com/ruman23/autoware.ai.git}

However, if we need to recompile the repository for any reason, all existing compiled files (except those in the "src" directory) must be cleared to prevent potential conflicts or issues. This can be done by deleting folders such as "make," "install," and "log." After this cleanup, the compilation can proceed using the same commands used for the original repository. This approach ensures a clean environment for a successful recompilation process.

\subsection{Run Autoware.AI}
There are several ways to use Autoware.AI, including running each node separately. The most convenient way to use the \texttt{runtime\_manager} is a GUI-based interface where we can easily simulate and control the testing AV. There are two ways to launch the \texttt{runtime\_manager} as explained below. \paragraph{Method I} In this method, we opened two terminals and set the source to run the ROS master and \texttt{runtime\_manager} in two different terminals.
\begin{enumerate}
\item First Terminal: We executed the command \texttt{roscore} to start the ROS master, a collection of essential services that manage communication and coordination between different ROS nodes. 
\item Second Terminal: After setting up the source in the second terminal, we continued and proceeded with the following command to launch the \texttt{runtime\_manager}. 
\begin{verbatim}
roslaunch runtime_manager runtime_manager.launch
\end{verbatim}
Note:  the \texttt{runtime\_manager} is a graphical interface used to manage and configure Autoware.AI. The .launch file will have instructions on how to start the \texttt{runtime\_manager} nodes and any other required nodes.
\end{enumerate}


\paragraph{Method II} While running the \texttt{runtime\_manager} following Method I, we faced issues with \texttt{proc\_manager}. \texttt{proc\_manager} in Autoware.AI is a component within the \texttt{runtime\_manager} responsible for managing and monitoring the execution of various processes and ROS nodes. It helps the \texttt{runtime\_manager} start, stop, and restart processes, ensuring the ADS runs smoothly and efficiently. This utility is important for maintaining the operational state of the autonomous driving system, allowing for easy control and management of different modules. Using proc manager, AV users can ensure that all necessary processes are active and that any issues during runtime are quickly addressed. 
\paragraph{Missing ssh-askpass} If there is an error about askpass, command \texttt{sudo apt-get install ssh-askpass} to install the \texttt{askpass} package. The \texttt{ssh-askpass} is installed only once to fix the sudo permission issue error. 

\paragraph{Missing jsk} Fix the error of \texttt{ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'jsk\_recognition\_msgs'} by installing the \texttt{jsk-recognition-msgs} package: 
\begin{verbatim}
sudo apt-get install ros-noetic-jsk-recognition-msgs
\end{verbatim}

\section{INTEGRATE VEHICLE INTERFACE TO AUTOWARE PLATFORM}
To use Autoware.AI for the simulation, we need the \texttt{ROSbag} data of the LiDAR sensors, Point Cloud Maps, Vector Maps, and Waypoints. We collected the LiDAR sensor data (in ROSbag format) using the testing AV, then generated the maps and waypoints using different tools that will be discussed later. The following demonstrates how we integrated Autoware.AI into the AV's DBW and SSC interface platform using LiDAR sensor data to generate the maps and waypoints.  

\subsection{LiDAR data collection}
The process of LiDAR and camera collection is different from that of the DBW and SSC interface environments. To collect LiDAR data using Autoware.AI, We first sourced the setup script to configure the environment. Then, we further launched the Velodyne LiDAR driver to start receiving point cloud data from the VLP-32C (LiDAR) sensor. Subsequently, we opened {\bf RViz} software to visualize the LiDAR data in real-time and finally, used the rosbag command (\texttt{rosbag record -a}) to record all ROS topics into a bag file for later analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:rosbag data}). The following commands launch LiDAR data collection and visualization in the Autoware.AI environment.

\begin{verbatim}
source install/setup.bash
roslaunch velodyne_pointcloud VLP-32C_points.launch
rosrun rviz rviz -f velodyne
rosbag record -a
\end{verbatim}

