    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{Figures/rosbag_data.png}
    \caption{Play rosbag data in RViz.} 
    \label{fig:rosbag data}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Downsample}
After completing the LiDAR data collection (in the rosbag format), the next step is to downsample the file using \texttt{voxel\_grid\_filter}. This downsample process is to improve the efficiency of Autoware.AI performance. The first step is to rename the collected .bag data as \texttt{bag\_name} and save it to the 'autoware.ai' folder. We continued and launched {\bf Runtime Manager} and loaded the rosbag data to review the rostopics (e.g., \texttt{velodyne\_points}) under the {\bf Simulation} tab followed by converting the \texttt{velodyne\_points} to \texttt{points\_raw} using the command \cite{tutorial} \texttt{rosbag play -r 0.7 bag\_name.bag /velodyne\_points:=/points\_raw}. In the Terminal, we can use SPACE to stop the downsampling process (Figure~\ref{fig:downsample}). This step is to enable the voxel grid filter from the Sensing tab to reduce the number of points in the point cloud, so enhancing the processing efficiency in Autoware.AI.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{Figures/downsample.png}
    \caption{An example of downsample process.} 
    \label{fig:downsample}
\end{figure}

Under {\bf ROSBAG Record}, we continued to click Refresh and check \texttt{points\_raw}. Then, rename the downsample file and save it in the desired location (e.g., Autoware.AI), and activate Start. Since we were converting the entire dataset, which means we were running the \texttt{bag\_name} data until it was completed, we will wait for the running process to be completed in the Terminal. Once enough data was yielded, we went to {\bf Runtime Manager} and clicked {\bf Stop} under \texttt{ROSBAG Record} to complete the process (Figure~\ref{fig:downsample}).

\subsection{Point cloud map collection}
We can use the following steps to collect the points cloud maps from the rosbag data.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Open the {\bf Simulation} tab and select the downsampling ROS bag file (Figure~\ref{fig:rosbag runtime}).
\item Click on the {\bf Play} button to start the simulation.
\item Enable the \texttt{voxel\_grid\_filter} from the Sensing tab
\item Navigate to Computing - lidar\_localizer - ndt\_matching to start the process (Figure~\ref{fig:ndt mapping}).
\item Open the application, select {\bf ref} to define the path, and click on {\bf PCD OUTPUT} to save the map in \texttt{ASCII} format
\item Autoware.AI supports binary-formatted point cloud maps. To convert the ASCII-formatted PCD file into binary, use the following command.
\begin{verbatim}
pcl_convert_pcd_ascii_binary source.pcd destination.pcd 1
\end{verbatim}
Where '1' represents the binary format. '0' for ASCII format, '2' for binary compressed format.
\item To view the PCD file format, use the command \texttt{head -n\textasciitilde20\textasciitilde file.pcd}
\end{enumerate}

