\section{Conclusion \& Future Work}

% \ch{this section can be cut by 2/3; just a short paragraph should be fine}

In this work, we introduce \textit{ForgeBench}, a benchmark suite tailored explicitly for next-generation HLS tools focused on machine learning architecture design. ForgeBench is accompanied by a user-friendly and highly extensible framework for automated HLS design generation and evaluation. We also provide two ready-to-use benchmarks; one includes over 6,000 representative ML test cases, and the other includes modular HLS test cases with possible resource sharing manually implemented to highlight the necessity of designing architecture-oriented HLS tools.

Existing research and recent advancements strongly support our vision for future architecture-oriented HLS tool design. Notably, equivalence-graphs (e-graphs) have shown promise for extracting shared structures across programs~\cite{BABBLE}, and have already proved effective at representing and optimizing hardware designs ~\cite{ROVER, SEER}. Consequently, we envision future HLS tools leveraging e-graphs to automatically identify common functional modules across HLS designs. After extracting common modules, the tools will generate a flexible finite-state machine and embed scheduling information into instructions that govern state transitions, enabling flexible dataflows to reuse functional modules. Furthermore, we foresee that HLS tools will perform design space exploration during architecture generation to determine the optimal module reuse choice, balancing area, and latency in a holistic and automated manner. Given these developments, and considering that ForgeBench is fully open-sourced and easily extensible, we believe it will substantially benefit ongoing and future HLS tools design and implementation.

% This work presents a machine learning benchmark suite and an automated HLS design generation framework tailored for next-generation HLS tool development and optimization. Our motivation stems from two key gaps. First, existing popular benchmark suites often rely on outdated algorithms and lack comprehensive HLS coverage for machine learning applications. Second, although current HLS tools are effective for accelerator design, they offer limited capabilities for architecture design. To support future development of HLS tools for architecture design, we develop a new HLS benchmark suite capable of generating modular HLS designs for ML applications, thereby facilitating robust evaluation and testing of future HLS tool development.

% We envision future HLS tools will support more flexible, general, and agile architecture generation processes, allowing more flexible and adaptive data flows and controls rather than fixed data flow accelerator designs. Since many ML models share a substantial number of functional modules, enhanced automation and general architecture design in HLS can enable significant reuse of these modules, thus significantly reducing design area rather than generating separate accelerators for each model. Furthermore, we foresee HLS tools performing design space exploration during architecture generation to determine an optimal tiling solution for more efficient functional modules reuse, balancing area and latency in a more holistic and automated manner.

% Existing research and recent advancements pave the way for future HLS tool design for architecture. Notably, equivalence-graphs (e-graphs) have shown promise for extracting shared structures across programs~\cite{BABBLE}, and have already proved effective at representing and optimizing hardware designs ~\cite{ROVER, SEER} via e-graph rewriting. Consequently, future HLS tools may leverage e-graphs to automatically discover common functional modules in multiple HLS programs for reuse. After identifying these modules, the tools could generate a flexible finite-state machine and embed scheduling information into instructions that govern state transitions, thereby enabling flexible dataflow designs to reuse functional modules. Pioneering works, such as TAPA~\cite{TAPA} and OverGen~\cite{OverGen}, highlight the promise of task-level scheduling and overlay generation, laying crucial groundwork for this next generation of HLS tools for architecture. Given these developments, and considering that our proposed benchmarking framework is fully open-sourced and easily extensible, we believe our benchmarking suite will substantially benefit ongoing and future HLS tool design and implementation.



