We now present our proposed \our{} framework as illustrated in \cref{fig:overview}. We introduce three key components for unified profile handling: Profile Embeddings (PE, \cref{sec:pe}),  Multi-Head Profiled Attention (MHPA, \cref{sec:mhpa}), and Profile-aware Score Reshaping (PSR, \cref{sec:psr}). Together, these components enable flexible adaptation to different profile weights and problem settings within a single model architecture. We then lay out the integration into the encoder-decoder framework (\cref{sec:framework-integration}) and the training scheme (\cref{sec:training}). 

\subsection{Profile Embeddings}
\label{sec:pe}
Previous approaches handle profiles by retraining separate models for each profile distribution, which is computationally inefficient and lacks flexibility. Our Profile Embeddings (PE) overcome this limitation by learning a unified representation that can encode any combination of attributes and profile weight parameters. 
Given an instance $\bm{x}$ and corresponding profile weight parameter $\alpha$, we first embed these features into a latent space $\bm{h}$ of size $d_h$ by considering separated contributions for clients, vehicles, profiles, and profile weights via linear layers parametrized by matrices $\mathbf{W}^{(\cdot)}_{\text{init}}$ and bias vectors $\mathbf{b}^{(\cdot)}_{\text{init}}$. 

\textit{Client Feature Embeddings} project client-specific information into a latent space:
%
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^c_i &= \mathbf{W}^c_{\text{init}}\bm{x}^c_i + \mathbf{b}^c_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}
\end{align}
%
where $\bm{x}^c_i\in\mathbb{R}^{d_c}$ contains client-specific features including demands and locations of size $d_c$, $\mathbf{W}^c_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h \times d_c}$, and $\mathbf{b}^c_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}$.

\textit{Vehicle Feature Embeddings} project vehicle-specific information:
%
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^v_j &= \mathbf{W}^v_{\text{init}}\bm{x}^v_j + \mathbf{b}^v_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}
\end{align}
%
where $\bm{x}^v_j\in\mathbb{R}^{d_v}$ contains vehicle-specific features of size $d_v$ including capacity and starting location, which practically encodes the depot information, $\mathbf{W}^v_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h \times d_v}$, and $\mathbf{b}^v_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}$.
%

\textit{Profile Score Embeddings} transform raw profile scores into learnable embeddings:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^p_{ij} &= \mathbf{W}^p_{\text{init}}p_{ij} + \mathbf{b}^p_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}
\end{align}
where $p_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}$ represents the profile between vehicle $i$ and client $j$, $\mathbf{W}^p_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h \times 1}$, and $\mathbf{b}^p_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}$.
%

\textit{Profile Weight Embeddings} encode objective weights to enable flexible tradeoffs:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^{\alpha} &= \mathbf{W}^{\alpha}_{\text{init}}\mathbf{\alpha} + \mathbf{b}^{\alpha}_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}
\end{align}
where $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$ represents the profile weights from \cref{eq:objective} which are then broadcasted on other embeddings, $\mathbf{W}^{\alpha}_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h \times 1}$, and $\mathbf{b}^{\alpha}_{\text{init}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_h}$.
PEs create a shared latent space that enables the model to handle arbitrary profile types without retraining. By projecting the client, vehicle, profiles, and profile weights, the model can simultaneously process profiles with varying magnitudes and values.


\subsection{Multi-Head Profiled Attention}
\label{sec:mhpa}
A fundamental limitation of existing approaches is their inability to capture rich bidirectional interactions between vehicles and clients with different profiles. To address this, we introduce Multi-Head Profiled Attention (MHPA) to allow for improved information exchange in each encoder layer:
%
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h} &= \text{Norm}(\text{MHPA}(\mathbf{h})) \\
    \mathbf{h} &= \text{Norm}(\text{FFN}(\mathbf{h}) + \mathbf{h})
\end{align}
%
where FFN denotes a multi-layer perceptron. MHPA is based on multi-head attention (MHA):
\begin{align}
    \text{MHA}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) &= \left( \mathop{\big\|}_{i=1}^{n_\text{heads}} \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{W}^Q_i, \mathbf{K}\mathbf{W}^K_i, \mathbf{V}\mathbf{W}^V_i) \right) \mathbf{W}^O 
\end{align}
%
with
%
\begin{align}
    \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) &= \text{softmax} \left( \frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^\top}{\sqrt{d_h}} \right) \mathbf{V}
\end{align}

MHPA improves on MHA for modeling PVRP with four distinct types of information exchange depending on the embedding:

\textit{1) Client-Client (CC) Attention} enables information sharing between clients about their spatial relationships and demands:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^{c'} &= \text{MHA}(\mathbf{h}^{c}, \mathbf{h}^{c}, \mathbf{h}^{c})
\end{align}

\textit{2) Vehicle-Vehicle (VV) Attention} facilitates communication between vehicles about their capacities and service capabilities:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^{v'} &= \text{MHA}(\mathbf{h}^{v}, \mathbf{h}^{v}, \mathbf{h}^{v})
\end{align}

\textit{3) Vehicle-Client (VC) Attention} enables vehicles to attend to relevant clients based on profiles:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^{pc'}_{i} &= \text{MHA}(\mathbf{h}^{v}, \mathbf{h}^{c}, \mathbf{h}^{pv}_{i})
\end{align}

\textit{4) Client-Vehicle (CV) Attention} allows clients to consider their suitability for different vehicles based on their profiles:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{h}^{pv'}_{i} &= \text{MHA}(\mathbf{h}^{c}, \mathbf{h}^{v}, \mathbf{h}^{pc}_{i})
\end{align}



Unlike previous approaches that only consider unidirectional interactions or simple aggregations, MHPA enables comprehensive information exchange through bidirectional attention mechanisms. The final MHPA output integrates all processed information:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{h}^{p'}_{ij} = \text{concat}(\mathbf{h}^{v}_i, \mathbf{h}^{c}_j, \mathbf{h}^{pv'}_{i}, \mathbf{h}^{pc'}_{j}) + \mathbf{h}^{p}_{ij}
\end{equation}

where each vehicle is assigned its own $\mathbf{h}$ processed latent representation. 


\subsection{Profile-aware Score Reshaping}
\label{sec:psr}
We further introduce Profile-aware Score Reshaping (PSR), which dynamically adjusts decoder logits to maintain robustness across varying profile distributions and out-of-distribution instances. Building on recent advancements in distance-based heuristics \citep{wang2024distance,huang2025rethinking}, PSR further combines learned embeddings with explicit profile and distance information:

\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{Z} = C \cdot \text{tanh}\left(\frac{\mathbf{U}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{ptr}}\mathbf{h})^\top}{\sqrt{d_h}} - \log(\text{dist}_{ij} + p_{ij})\right)
\end{equation}

where $C$ is a scaling factor set to $10$ following \citet{bello2016neural}, $\mathbf{U} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times d_h}$ represents the decoder's query vectors, $\mathbf{W}_{\text{ptr}}$ projects node embeddings into the pointer space, $\text{dist}_{ij}$ is the distance between nodes $i$ and $j$, and $p_{ij}$ is the preference score between vehicle $i$ and client $j$\footnote{We note that while we manually design the attention reshaping mechanism in this paper, we could leverage automatic algorithm design \citep{liu2024evolution,ye2024reevo,pham2025hsevo,tranlarge,zhao2025trajevodesigningtrajectoryprediction} to do it, which we leave as future work.}.

PSR provides several key advantages: it balances the flexibility of neural approaches with the reliability of traditional heuristics; naturally penalizes nodes that are either spatially distant or have low preference scores; ensures consistent performance across varying profile distributions and out-of-distribution instances; and enables smooth adaptation without retraining when preference weights change. The final action probabilities are computed by masking infeasible actions and applying softmax:
\begin{equation}
    P(\mathbf{a}_t | \mathbf{s}_t) = \text{softmax}(\mathbf{Z} + \mathbf{M}_t)
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{M}_t$ is the mask tensor for infeasible actions at step $t$.

\subsection{Integration into Encoder-Decoder Framework}
\label{sec:framework-integration}
The three components described above are integrated into an encoder-decoder framework \citep{hua2025camp_vrp}. The encoder $f_\theta(\bm{x},\alpha)$ first processes raw problem features using PE to create initial embeddings as in \cref{sec:pe}, then applies encoder layers with MHPA from \cref{sec:mhpa} to capture complex interactions between clients and vehicles. The encoder outputs a set of embeddings $\mathbf{h} = [\mathbf{h}^{1}, \ldots, \mathbf{h}^{M}]$, where each $\mathbf{h}^k \in \mathbb{R}^{(M+N) \times d_h}$ represents the encoded graph information for vehicle profile $k$.

The decoder $g_\theta$ generates vehicle-specific queries that capture both profile and current state information:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{q}_t^k = \mathbf{W}_{\text{query}}[\mathbf{h}^k_k \| \mathbf{h}^k_{\text{cur}} \| \mathbf{W}_{\text{state}}\mathbf{s}_t^k]
\end{equation}
where $\|\cdot\|$ denotes concatenation, $\mathbf{h}^k_k$ is the vehicle's profile embedding, $\mathbf{h}^k_{\text{cur}}$ represents the current node embedding, and $\mathbf{s}_t^k$ captures the state features at time $t$.
%
% \begin{align}
%     \mathbf{H}_t &= \text{Norm}(\text{MHA}(\mathbf{Q}_t, \mathbf{Q}_t, \mathbf{Q}_t) + \mathbf{Q}_t) \\
%     \mathbf{Q}'_t &= \text{Norm}(\text{MLP}(\mathbf{H}_t) + \mathbf{H}_t)
% \end{align}
%
We then process the multiple vehicle queries $\mathbf{q}_t = [\mathbf{q}_t^1, \dots, \mathbf{q}_t^M]$ via a single communication layer \citep{berto2024parco,hua2025camp_vrp} -- common also outside of VRPs \citep{tang2024himap,tang2024ensembling} -- composed of standard MHA and FFN, and then these are fed into a multiple pointer mechanism that generates the decoder output $\mathbf{U}$. Finally, PSR is applied to compute action probabilities for each vehicle as in \cref{sec:psr}.


\subsection{Training}
\label{sec:training}
We train our model using the REINFORCE algorithm with a shared baseline \citep{kim2022sym} across all agents. During training, we sample $\alpha_i$ from a predefined distribution of profile weights $\mathcal{D}_\alpha = [\alpha_{min}, \alpha_{max}]$ for each instance in the batch. This allows the model to learn a unified policy across different preference-cost trade-offs.

The policy gradient is estimated as:

\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:reinforce}
    \begin{split}
        \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{B \cdot L} \sum_{i=1}^{B} \sum_{j=1}^{L} 
        &(R(\bm{x}_i, \bm{a}_{ij}, \alpha_i) - b^{\text{shared}}(\bm{x}_i)) \\
        &\cdot \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(\bm{a}_{ij} | \bm{x}_i, \alpha_i)
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
where $B$ is the batch size, $L$ is the number of solutions per instance, and $b^{\text{shared}}$ is the shared baseline value obtained through symmetric augmentation sampling.











