\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
For polynomials $p(x)$ in $D \in \mathbb{N}$ dimensions of degree at most $d \in \mathbb{N}$, consider  global non-convex polynomial optimization problems of the form,
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:problemcontinuous}
    \min_{[-1,1]^D} & \;\; p(x).
\end{align}

Based on an evolution of the ideas proposed by Lasserre in~\cite{lasserre2001global}, we have developed a novel theoretical and numerical framework for efficiently solving problems in the form of~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous}; specifically, such problems can be efficiently reformulated as problems involving a non-linear objective over a convex cone of semi-definite matrices in $O(D\cdot d^2)$ dimensions.  Further, our approach possesses no spurious local minima (i.e., every local minimum is a global minimum).  Our numerical framework for solving the non-convex polynomial optimization problems from~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous} is based on the augmented Lagrangian and ``Burer-Monteiro Method'' \cite{burer2003nonlinear} for expressing semi-definite constraints efficiently. 

Our contributions can be summarized as:
\begin{itemize}
    \item {\bf Efficient reformulations of global polynomial optimization problems}: a reformulation of~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous} is introduced as a problem involving a canonical non-linear objective over a cone of semi-definite matrices. Both formulations share the same optimal value (shown in Theorem \ref{thm:equivalence}) and location (discussed in Section~\ref{sec:optimal_location}).
    \item {\bf Efficient characterization of product measures}: a complete and efficient characterization is constructed of the set of product measures supported over $[-1,1]^D$ through their moments using semi-definite constraints (shown in Proposition \ref{prop:measureextension}).
     \item {\bf Efficient numerical algorithms}: an efficient algorithmic framework and numerical implementation prototype is provided for solving our efficient reformulation of ~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous} with efficient computational complexity (discussed in Section~\ref{sec:algorithm}).  Through examples, we demonstrate polynomial scaling on previously intractable global optimization problems (shown for dimensions up to $250$ in Section~\ref{sec:numerical_results}). 
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Previous work is discussed in Section~\ref{subsec:previous_work}.  Section~\ref{sec:theory} introduces notation, theory, and our novel reformulation of~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous}. Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} discusses algorithmic and numerical aspects associated with the computational treatment our reformulation. Section~\ref{sec:numerical_results} presents numerical results demonstrating correctness and performance of our framework. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} presents conclusions and a discussion of future work. Proofs can be found in Appendix~\ref{sec:proofs}.

\subsection{Previous Work}
\label{subsec:previous_work}
Our reformulation and efficient framework for solving problems of the form~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous} is inspired by the polynomial optimization framework introduced by 
Lasserre~\cite{lasserre2001global}, who proposed to reformulate~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous} through a Semi-Definite Program (SDP). The primary advantage of this approach is that the resulting convex problem can be solved more efficiently than the original formulation. However, rather than provide an exact reformulation,~\cite{lasserre2001global} results in a convex relaxation, where the solution provides a {\em lower bound} on the optimal minimum.  The quality of this bound depends on the degree of the Sums-Of-Squares (SOS)~\cite{doi:10.1137/040614141,prajna2002introducing} polynomials used to generate the relaxation and; this ultimately affects the size of the resulting SDP, adversely affecting scaling. In~\cite{lasserre2001global,Parrilo2003} it was demonstrated that any sequence of problems of increasing SOS degree (also known as the ``Lasserre Hierarchy'') will eventually converge to an exact solution in finite time. However, although this hierarchy has been found to converge relatively rapidly on some specific problems (see, e.g., \cite{de2022convergence}), theoretical estimates indicate that super-exponential sizes are required in the worst case~\cite{Klerk2011}.

Proofs of convergence and degree estimates for the approach of~\cite{lasserre2001global} rely on results by Pitunar~\cite{putinar1993positive})(``Putinar Positivstellensatz'') and Nie and Schweighofer~\cite{nie2007complexity}. The results of~\cite{putinar1993positive,nie2007complexity} address the problem of expressing positive polynomials over compact semi-algebraic subsets of Euclidean spaces through SOS, or SOS-like, representations of finite degree. They demonstrate that this can be done in any dimension; however, their estimates demand a super-exponential SOS degree of the original problem in both dimension and degree, resulting in intractable estimates for the cost of solving polynomial optimization problems using~\cite{lasserre2001global}'s framework in the general case. 

{\em Our framework and results overcome the hurdles of~\cite{lasserre2001global}} by restricting the space of positive polynomials (and measures) under consideration to that of sums of products of one-dimensional polynomials positive over $[-1,1]$. In fact, Proposition~\ref{prop:measureextension} demonstrates that such polynomials \emph{can} be represented efficiently through an SOS representation (specifically \emph{linear} SOS degree in dimension and degree of the original problem).  In this restricted case, the moment problem \footnote{i.e., the problem of extending elements of the dual space to positive product measures} can be characterized and solved efficiently, requiring the knowledge of only linearly many moments in the dimension and degree of the original problem.  Such conclusions are much stronger than the general case proposed by~\cite{lasserre2001global} and highlight the efficiency of our novel reformulation.

% It is well-known that not all positive polynomials may be represented in such form in dimension larger than $1$
%  (see, e.g., \cite{}). 

From an algorithmic standpoint, it is well-known that SDPs are tractable~\cite{DBLP:books/daglib/0071613,LAURENT2005393} (when the Slater constraint qualification is satisfied). Indeed, being convex problems, SDPs may be solved in polynomial time~\cite{Bubeck,ben2001lectures}. Despite such characteristics, however, computing the solution of an SDP using existing numerical solvers often carry a prohibitively high cost in practice~\cite{KANNAN2019351, Klerk2011}. For example, using an eigenvalue decomposition for enforcing positive-definiteness may carry a cost of $O(N^3)$ where $N$ is the number of primal variables~\cite{golub2013matrix}; this computational cost is prohibitively high for many interesting problems arising from the Lasserre framework. In practice, semi-definite relaxations are often solved using interior-point methods (e.g., \cite{andersen2011interior,grant2011cvx,DBLP:books/daglib/0071613}). Modern implementations, however, are limited to problems of relatively small sizes due to their associated computational costs~\cite{Mittleman}, especially for increasing interest in problems that are large-scale and potentially ill-posed.~\cite{Fujisawa}

One recent approach that reduces this cost is that or Burer and Monteiro (BM, \cite{burer2003nonlinear}) who proposed to replace semi-definite constraints of the form $A \succeq 0$ by equality constraints of the form: $A = R \, R^T$. Boumal \cite{boumal2016non} showed that when the rank of $R$ is large enough (i.e., roughly the square root of the number of constraints in dual form), the resulting (non-linear) BM problem possess the property that every local minimum is a global minimum (second-order stationarity is, in fact, necessary and sufficient). When the rank of $R$ is appropriately small, this leads to linear scaling estimates for pragmatically solving SDPs.  Another recent approach for mitigating the restrictions of large SDPs is presented by Dressler et. al in ~\cite{Dressler2017}.  The authors present a Positivstellensatz based on sums of nonnegative circuit polynomials (SONC), which coupled with geometric programming (GP) allows for computing faster lower bounds than Lassere's SOS/SDP approach on certain classes of problems.  Further, for a polynomial that is sparse and SONC, there is a guarantee of a certificate with the same sparsity.

While the approach of Dressler et. al~\cite{Dressler2017} is a promising new approach, leveraging BM~\cite{burer2003nonlinear} allows for potentially greater generality for larger classes of polynomial optimization. Polynomials optimization problems on compact sets can be tackled exactly using the BM approach within the Laserre SOS framework to enforce semi-definite constraints.  By reformulating problems of the form in Equation~\eqref{eq:problemcontinuous}, we can solve the resulting formulations using BM and the augmented Lagrangian.  More details are discussed in Sections~\ref{sec:theory}-~\ref{sec:algorithm}.

\section{Theory}
\label{sec:theory}

In Section~\ref{sec:notation}, we introduce the notation used throughout the remainder of the paper.  In Section \ref{sec:reformulation_theory}, we present our re-formulation and theoretical results demonstrating correctness and efficiency.

\subsection{Notation}
\label{sec:notation}
We represent the \emph{dimension} of problem~\ref{eq:problemcontinuous} as $D \in \mathbb{N}$, and $d$ to represent the \emph{problem degree} (i.e., the degree of the objective polynomial). A $D$-dimensional \emph{multi-index} $n=(n_1, n_2, ..., n_D)$ is an element of $\mathbb{N}^D$, where $\lvert n \rvert = \sum_{i=1}^D n_i$ refers to the \emph{multi-index degree}. Unless otherwise stated, a basis of \emph{monomials} of the form,
\begin{equation}
    x^n = \prod_{i=1}^D x_i^{n_i}
\end{equation}
is used to represent elements of $\mathbb{P}_{D,d}$, the vector space of polynomials of degree $d$ in dimension $D$; i.e., 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P}_{D,d} := \left \{ \sum_{\lvert n \rvert \leq d} \, p_n \, x^n : \, p_n \in \mathbb{R} \; \forall \; n \in \mathbb{N}^D \, \mathrm{s.t.} \, \lvert n \rvert \leq d \right \},
\end{equation}
where the short-hand notation 
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{\lvert n \rvert \leq d} a_n= \sum_{ n \in \{ n : \lvert n \rvert \leq d \} } a_{(n_1, n_2, ..., n_D)}
\end{equation*}
represents summations over all multi-indices of degree less than or equal to objective polynomial degree $d$.  In particular, note that $\mathbb{P}_{D,d}$ has dimension
\begin{equation}
    \# \{ n \in \mathbb{N}^D \, : \, \lvert n \rvert \leq d \} = \binom{D+d}{d} \leq \min \left ( D^d, d^D \right ),
\end{equation}
Hence, for a fixed degree $d$, the dimension of the vector space of polynomials space $\mathbb{P}_{D,d}$ grows {\emph polynomially} in the problem dimension $D$. The notation $\mathbb{P}_{D,\infty}$ is used to represent polynomials of any finite degree. The \emph{support of a polynomial} $p \in \mathbb{P}_{D,\infty}$ corresponds to the locations of its nonzero coefficients; i.e.,
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{supp}(p) := \{ n \in \mathbb{N}^D \, : \, p_n \not = 0 \}, 
\end{equation}
and the cardinality (number of elements) of the support is denoted by
$$N_p := \# \, \mathrm{supp}(p).$$

The set of regular Borel measures (see, e.g., \cite{cohn2013measure}) over $\mathbb{R}^D$ is denoted as $\mathbb{B}_{D}$, and
the \emph{support of a measure} $\mu(\cdot) \in \mathbb{B}_D$ is designated by $\mathrm{supp}(\mu(\cdot))$, representing the smallest set such that $\mu \left ( \mathbb{R}^D \backslash \mathrm{supp}(\mu(\cdot)) \right )  = 0$. 
% , i.e.,
% \begin{equation}
%    \mathbb{B}_{D} := \left \{  \mu(\cdot) \, : \, \right \} 
% \end{equation}
%A finite regular Borel measure $\mu(\cdot)$ over $\mathbb{R}^D$ is a positive measure over the Borel $\sigma$-algebra $\mathbb{B}(\mathbb{R}^D)$ defined over $\mathbb{R}^D$ that satisfies $\mu(\mathbb{R}^D) < \infty$ and,\footnote{Please refer to \cite{cohn2013measure} for further details.}
% \begin{align}
%     \mu(S) &= \inf \left \{ \mu(O) \, : \, S \subset O,\, O \, \mathrm{open} \right \}\\
%     &= \sup \left \{ \mu(C) \, : \, C \subset S,\, C \, \mathrm{compact \, and \, Borel} \right \}
% \end{align}
% for every Borel set $S$. 
% We will use the symbol $\mathbb{B}_D$ to represents the set of finite regular Borel measures in $D$ dimensions supported over $[-1,1]^D$. 
Given such a measure and a fixed multi-index $n$, its $n^{th}$ \emph{moment} is defined to be the multi-dimensional Lebesgue integral,
\begin{equation}
    \mu_n := \int_{\mathbb{R}^D} x^n \, \mathrm{d} \mu(x).
\end{equation}
A \emph{regular product measure} is a regular Borel measures of the form,
\begin{equation}
    \mu(\cdot) = \prod_{i=1}^D  \mu_i(\cdot) ,
\end{equation}
where each factor $\mu_i(\cdot)$ belongs to $\mathbb{B}_1$. In this context, the \emph{moments of a product measure} up to degree $d$ correspond to a $D$-tuple of real vectors $( \mu_1, \mu_2, ..., \mu_D ) \in \mathbb{R}^{(2d+1) \times D}$ where each element is defined as
\begin{equation}
    \mu_{i,n_i} := \int_{\mathbb{R} } x_i^{n_i} \, \mathrm{d} \mu_i (x_i).
\end{equation}
The vectors $\mu_i$ refer to the vector of \emph{moments of the 1D measure} $\mu_i$.  The notation $\mu_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d+1}$ with a single sub-index represents the vector of moments, and $\mu_{i,n_i} \in \mathbb{R}$ with two sub-indices represents scalar moments (vector elements) interchangeably when the context is clear.  The symbol $\mu = \{\mu_i^{(l)}\}_{l,i} \in \mathbb{R}^{(d+1) \times D \times L}$ represents a set of $L$ moments of product measures. The presence of parenthesis (e.g., $\mu_i(\cdot)$) will always indicate that we are dealing with a measure, whereas the absence of parentheses indicates that we are dealing with vectors. 

Given a sequence of moments $\mu_i$ of a 1D measure, we define the \emph{degree-$d$ 1D moment matrix} (or just \emph{moment matrix} when the context is clear) associated with the one-dimensional polynomial $g(x_i) \in \mathbb{P}_{1,d^\prime}$, $0 \leq d^\prime < \infty$ to be the $(d +1) \times (d +1) $ matrix $\mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i, g(x_i))$ with elements,
 \begin{equation}
     [ \mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i, g(x)) ]_{m_i,n_i} = \int g(x_i) \, x_i^{m_i + n_i} \, \mathrm{d} \mu_i(x_i) = \sum_{k_i=0}^{d^\prime} g_{k_i} \, \mu_{k_i+m_i+n_i}
 \end{equation}
for $0 \leq m_i, n_i \leq d $. When $g(\cdot ) \equiv 1$, we shall use the short-hand notation,
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i; 1) = \mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i)
\end{equation}
In particular, we note that the construction of such matrices requires the knowledge of only the first $2 d+ d^\prime + 1$ moments of $\mu_i (\cdot) $, i.e., $\{ \mu_{i,n_i}\}_{n_i=0}^{2d + d^\prime} $.

\subsection{Efficient Re-formulation}
\label{sec:reformulation_theory}
Given some integer $L$, our proposed efficient re-formulation takes the form,
% \begin{align}
%     \label{eq:reformulationcontinuous}
%     \min_{  \{ \mu_i^{(l)} \}_{i,l=1}^{D,L} \in \mathbb{R}^{  (d+1) \times D \times L}  }&\;\; \sum_{n \in \mathrm{supp}(p)} \, p_n \left ( \sum_{l=1}^L  \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} \right )\\
%     \mathrm{subject\, to} &\;\; \mathcal{M}_d (\mu^{(l)}_i) \succeq 0, \\
%     &\;\; \mathcal{M}_{d-1} (\mu^{(l)}_i; \, 1-x_i^2) \succeq 0  \;& \forall i=1,..., D, l=1,..., L \\
%     & \;\; \sum_{l=1}^L \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(l)}_{i,0} = 1, %\,  0 \leq \alpha_l \leq 1 \; 
% \end{align} 

\begin{align}
    \label{eq:reformulationcontinuous}
    \min_{  \mu \in \mathbb{R}^{(d+1)\times D \times L} }&\;\; \sum_{n \in \mathrm{supp}(p)} \, p_n \, \phi_n (\mu) \nonumber \\
    \mathrm{subject\, to} &  \;\; \mathcal{M}_d (\mu^{(l)}_i) \succeq 0,  \\
    &\;\; \mathcal{M}_{d-1} (\mu^{(l)}_i; \, 1-x_i^2) \succeq 0,  \;&  i=1,..., D, l=1,..., L \nonumber \\
    & \;\; \mu^{(l)}_{i,0} \geq 0 \nonumber \\
    &\;\; \phi_{(0,...,0)}  (\mu)  = 1, \nonumber  \\
    & \;\;  \phi_n (\mu)  = \sum_{l=1}^L  \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i}. \nonumber
\end{align} 

This corresponds to a nonlinear objective subject to (convex) semi-definite constraints. The re-formulation itself is deemed efficient because the feasible set lies in a space of dimension $L \cdot D \cdot (d + 1) $, which is linear in both degree and dimension (see Table \ref{tab:cost} for more details on computational costs).

The key advantages of this re-formulation can be found below in Theorem \ref{thm:equivalence} and Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin}; Theorem \ref{thm:equivalence} demonstrates the equivalence between the original and re-formulated problem, while Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin} demonstrates that the re-formulated problem may be solved efficiently using local descent techniques only.

The proof of Theorem \ref{thm:equivalence} relies on Proposition \ref{prop:measureextension} which is an interesting result in itself. Indeed, it demonstrates the possibility of efficiently (i.e., at a polynomial cost) extending linear functionals belonging to the dual space $\mathbb{P}_{D,d}^*$ when the latter are in \emph{product form}. This is in contrast with the general case which rules out such extension, a consequence related to the fact that positive polynomials cannot generally be written as combination of sums-of-squares having a sufficiently small degree (see, e.g., Motzkin polynomial \cite{theodore1965motzkin}). This underlies key differences between this work and that introduced by Lasserre (e.g., \cite{lasserre2009moments}) for instance. Indeed, in Lasserre's work the lack of existence of an efficient representation leads to re-formulations (i.e., semi-definite relaxations) that provide a \emph{lower bound}. To obtain a solution to the original problem under Lasserre's framework, one needs to consider a \emph{hierarchy of problems} (the commonly-called Lasserre hierarchy) of increasing (and potentially exponential) size. As we show below, our proposed framework does not suffer from such drawbacks; the dimension of the re-formulated problem is fixed and of size at most polynomial in dimension and degree, the optimal value obtained matches that of the original problem exactly, and no hierachy is required. 


\begin{restatable}{proposition}{measureextension}
    \label{prop:measureextension}
    Let $D,d \in \mathbb{N}$ and $( \mu_1, \mu_2, ..., \mu_D ) \in \mathbb{R}^{(d+1) \times D}$ be such that for each $i = 1, ..., D$, $\mu_{i,0} = 1$ and,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i) &\succeq 0,\\
        \mathcal{M}_{d-1}(\mu_i;  1-x_i^2) &\succeq 0 .
    \end{align*}
    Then, there exists a regular Borel product measure,
    \begin{equation*}
        \mu( \cdot ) := \prod_{i=1}^D  \mu_i(\cdot), 
    \end{equation*}
    supported over $[-1,1]^D$ such that $\mu \left ( [-1,1]^D \right )  = 1$ and,
    \begin{equation*}
         \int_{[-1,1]^D} \, x^n \, \left (  \prod_{i=1}^D \mu_i(x_i) \right ) \, \mathrm{d} x =  \prod_{i=1}^D \mu_{i,n_i},
    \end{equation*}
    for all multi-index $n \in \mathbb{N}^D$.
    % such that $ 0\leq n_i \leq d \; \forall \; i$
    % \begin{equation}
    %     \mu([-1,1]^D) = \prod_{i=1}^D \mu_{i,0} = 1
    % \end{equation}
\end{restatable}
In particular, Proposition \ref{prop:measureextension} states that the feasible set of Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous} corresponds to a set of \emph{convex combination of product measures} supported over the hypercube $[-1,1]^D$. This, in turn, implies the following results,
\begin{restatable}{theorem}{equivalence}
    \label{thm:equivalence}
    Problem \ref{eq:problemcontinuous} and Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous} share the same global minimum value.
\end{restatable}
\begin{restatable}{theorem}{globalmin}
    \label{thm:globalmin}
    Let $ \left \{  \mu_i^{(0)},\mu_i^{(1)}  \right \}_{i=1}^{D} \in \mathbb{R}^{(d+1) \times D \times 2 }  $ be a feasible point of Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous} corresponding to a local minimum, and let $\alpha^{(j)} = \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(j)}_{i,0}$
    % \begin{equation}
    %     \alpha^{(j)} = \int_{[-1,1]^D} \mathrm{d} \left ( \prod_{i=1}^{D} \mu_i^{(j)} (x_i) \right )  
    % \end{equation}
    for $j \in \{0,1\}$. Then, such a point corresponds to a global minimum if and only if $\alpha^{(0)} \not = 0$ and the point,
    $$\left \{  \frac{\mu^{(0)}_i }{\alpha^{(0)}} , 0 \right \}_{i=1}^{D},$$
    is a local minimum, or $\alpha^{(1)} \not = 0$ and the point,
    $$\left \{ 0, \frac{\mu^{(1)}_i}{\alpha^{(1)}} \right \}_{i=1}^{D}$$
    is a local minimum.
\end{restatable}
Theorem \ref{thm:equivalence} states that it is possible to solve Problem \ref{eq:problemcontinuous} by solving Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous}, while Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin} states that, for all practical purposes, every local minimum of Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous} is a global minimum. Indeed, using Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin} one can devise a descent algorithm for computing a local minimum of Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous} (see Section \ref{sec:algorithm}). Following such a computation, there must exists (by construction) at least one of $\alpha^{(0)}$ or $\alpha^{(1)}$ which is nonzero. In this case, one may substitute the corresponding point specified in Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin} and re-launch the descent algorithm. If the algorithm fails to descend, it implies that the new point is a local minimum and one concludes that the original point is a global minimum. Otherwise, another local minimum with lower value will be computed and one may repeat the above process until convergence to a global minimum.

Finally, even though Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin} is stated using a combination of only two ($L=2$) product measures, the conclusion still holds for any $L \geq 2$; the choice of $L=2$ was made for the purpose of simplifying the analysis. In practice, it is common to set $2 \leq L \leq 20$ (see Section \ref{sec:numerical_results}). Adding more product measures increases the number of degrees of freedom and often improves convergence of the numerical algorithm.



