\section{Algorithm}
\label{sec:algorithm}
Theorem \ref{thm:equivalence} demonstrates the possibility of recovering the optimal value of Problem \ref{eq:problemcontinuous} by solving Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous}. In addition, Theorem \ref{thm:globalmin} shows that such optimal values may be reached using local descent methods only. In this light, we have implemented an algorithm for solving the re-formulated problem based on such techniques. This section provides details about our algorithm. Technical details peculiar to our implementation can be found in Section \ref{sec:algdetails}. Numerical results are discussed in Section \ref{sec:numerical_results}.

The proposed algorithm is an extension of the method of Burer and Monteiro (BM) presented in \cite{burer2003nonlinear}. The main idea behind the BM method is to replace positive-definite constraints of the form $A \succeq 0$ by constraints of the form: $A = R R^T$ for some matrix $R$ of appropriate size. In this context, Problem \ref{eq:problemcontinuous} can be written as,
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:reformulationcontinuous_BM}
    \min_{  \mu \in \mathbb{R}^{  (d+1) \times D \times L}   }&\;\; \sum_{n \in \mathrm{supp}(p)} \, p_n \, \phi_n (\mu)\nonumber \\
    \mathrm{subject\, to} & \;\; \mathcal{M}_d(\mu^{(l)}_i) = R^{(l)}_i R^{(l)^T}_i ,  \\
    &\;\; \mathcal{M}_{d-1}(\mu^{(l)}_i; \, 1-x_i^2) = S^{(l)}_i S^{(l)^T}_i , \; & i=1,..., D, l=1,..., L \nonumber\\
    &\;\; R_{i}^{(l)} \in \mathbb{R}^{(d+1) \times (d+1)} , \, S_{i}^{(l)}  \in \mathbb{R}^{ d \times d }, \nonumber\\
    & \; \; \mu_{i,0} \geq 0, \nonumber \\
    & \;\;  \phi_{(0,...,0)} (\mu) = 1, \nonumber \\
    & \;\;   \phi_n (\mu) =  \sum_{l=1}^L  \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} .\nonumber
\end{align}
In particular, in order to simplify the implementation, our numerical implementation fixes $\mu^{(l)}_{i,0} = 1$ for $i\geq 2$, while allowing $\mu^{(l)}_{0,0}$ to remain a variable. Upon re-expressing the matrix element-wise equality constraints as a sequence of $K = L D ( (d+1)^2 + d^2 + 1) + 1$ scalar equality constraints, we obtain a problem of the form,
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:reformulationcontinuous_BM_general}
    \min_{ \mu \in \mathbb{R}^{  (d+1) \times D \times L}   }&\;\; \sum_{n \in \mathrm{supp}(p)} \, p_n \, \phi_n(\mu) \\
    %\left ( \sum_{l=1}^L  \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} \right )\\
    \mathrm{subject\, to} &\;\; c_k\left ( \mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\} \right ) = 0 , &\;\;  \, k=1,..., K \nonumber
\end{align}
This is the form we consider for numerical optimization. To solve this problem numerically, our implementation constructs the augmented Lagrangian as proposed in \cite{burer2003nonlinear}. The augmented Lagrangian takes the form,
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:augmented_lagrangian}
    \mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\}; \lambda) &:=  \sum_{n \in \mathrm{supp}(p)} \, p_n \, \phi_n(\mu) +  \sum_{k=1}^K \lambda_k \cdot c_k\left ( \mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\} \right ) \\
    &+ \frac{\gamma}{2} \, \sum_{k=1}^K  c^2_k\left ( \mu, \{R^{(l)}, S^{(l)}\} \right ) \nonumber
\end{align}
where $\gamma > 0$ is a penalty parameter. This is similar to the Lagrangian introduced in \cite{burer2003nonlinear}, with the difference that the objective exhibits non-linearities.

We solve this problem numerically using a technique akin to that proposed by Boyd et al. \cite{boyd2011distributed}, which involves finding a saddle-point (i.e., a simultaneous minimum and maximum in the primal and dual variables respectively) to the augmented Lagrangian, i.e., finding $\mu^*$ and $\lambda^*$ such that,
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu^*, \{R_i^{(l)^*}, S_i^{(l)^*}\}; \lambda^*) = \max_\lambda \,\min_{\mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\}}   \mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\} ; \lambda)
\end{equation}
Specifically, we alternatively solve the \emph{primal} and \emph{dual} problems until convergence. on one hand, the primal problem takes the form,
\begin{equation}
    \min_{\mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\}} \, \mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu, \{R^{(l)}, S^{(l)}\}; \lambda)
\end{equation}
where the Lagrange multipliers (dual variables) $\lambda$ are fixed. This is an unconstrained optimization problem with an objective that is bounded below (thanks to the penalty term). We can thus solve the latter using a descent technique. For this purpose, our algorithm uses L-BFGS \cite{liu1989limited}. On the other hand, the dual problem can be written as,
\begin{equation}
    \max_{\lambda} \, \mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu, \{R^{(l)}, S^{(l)}\}; \lambda)
\end{equation}
where the primal variables $\mu$,  $R_i^{(l)}$  and $S_i^{(l)}$ are fixed. Because this problem is generally unbounded at non-optimal points, we use an update rule proposed by \cite{boyd2011distributed} which has the form,
\begin{equation}
    \lambda_{\mathrm{next}} = \lambda_{\mathrm{current}} + \gamma \, \nabla_{\lambda} \,\mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\}; \lambda)   = \lambda_{\mathrm{current}} + \gamma \, \sum_{k=1}^K  c^2_k(\mu, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\})
\end{equation}
This process is summarized in Algorithm \ref{alg:alternating_saddle_point}. We have implemented this algorithm in the C/C++ language. Section \ref{sec:numerical_results} presents numerical results.

\begin{algorithm} 
\caption{Alternating descent/ascent algorithm for computing a saddle-point of the augmented Lagrangian}
\label{alg:alternating_saddle_point} 
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \Procedure{AlternatingSaddlePoint}{$\mu_0, \lambda_0; \gamma$}
        \State $\mu \gets \mu_0$
        \State $\lambda \gets \lambda_0$
        \While{Not converged} %\Comment{We have the answer if r is 0}
            \State $\mu \gets \mathrm{argmin}_{\mu} \, \mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu; \lambda)$\Comment{Using L-BFGS}
            \State $\lambda \gets \lambda + \gamma \, \nabla_{\lambda} \,\mathcal{L}_\gamma (\mu; \lambda) $
        \EndWhile\label{euclidendwhile}
        \State \textbf{return} $(\mu^*,\lambda^*)$\Comment{saddle-point}
    \EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\subsection{Optimal Location}
\label{sec:optimal_location}
When the global minimum $x^* \in [-1,1]^D$ of Problem \ref{eq:problemcontinuous} is unique, it can be shown that the solution of the re-formulated problem (Problem \ref{eq:reformulationcontinuous}) is also unique and must consists in the convex combinations of delta measures centered at the point $x^*$. In other words, under these conditions, one finds that,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} }{\prod_{i=1}^D \mu_{i,0}^{(l)} } = \int_{-1}^1 x_i^{n_i} \,  \delta(x_i - x_i^*) \, \mathrm{d} x_i = (x_i^*)^{n_i},
\end{equation}
for every fixed index $l$ such that $ \mu_{i,0}^{(l)} \not = 0 $ for every $i$. Therefore, in this special case, the above scheme does not merely provide a framework for finding the global optimal value, but also provides a way of computing a globally-optimal location. Indeed, it suffices to pick any $l$ such the above condition holds and then compute,
\begin{equation}
    x_i^* = \frac{\mu^{(l)}_{i,1} }{ \prod_{i=1}^D  \mu_{i,0}^{(l)} }
\end{equation}
for each $i = 1, ..., D$. This is summarized in Algorithm \ref{alg:solution_extraction}. \\

\begin{algorithm} 
\caption{Optimal location extraction (assuming unique global minimum) }
\label{alg:solution_extraction} 
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \Procedure{OptimalLocation}{$\mu$}\Comment{Input is output of Algorithm \ref{alg:alternating_saddle_point} }
        \State$l^* \gets \mathrm{argmax}_{l=1,...,L} \, \prod_{i=1}^D \mu_{i,0}^{(l)} $
        \For{$i = 1, ..., D$} 
            \State $ x_i^* \gets \frac{\mu^{(l^*)}_{i,1} }{\prod_{i=1}^D \mu_{i,0}^{(l^*)} } $
        \EndFor
        \State \textbf{return} $x^*$\Comment{Global minimum location}
    \EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

When the global mimimum is not unique (or not known to be so), one may add a small amount of noise to the objective and make it so with high probability. Other more accurate (and more technically involved) techniques may also be used in this case, although this is outside the scope of this paper.

\subsection{Further Implementation details}
\label{sec:algdetails}
This section is intended to refine the information provided in Section \ref{sec:algorithm} and discusses in greater details technicalities specific to our implementation. 

\subsubsection{Constraint simplification}
Our re-formulation dictates that the semi-definite constraints should be expressed as,
\begin{align*}
    &\mathcal{M}_d(\mu^{(l)}_i) \succeq 0,  \\
    & \mathcal{M}_{d-1}(\mu^{(l)}_i; \, 1-x_i^2) \succeq 0
\end{align*}
for $l=1, ..., L$ and $i=1,...,D$. Within our implementation, however, we use the equivalent constraints,
\begin{align*}
    &\mathcal{M}_d(\mu^{(l)}_i) \succeq 0, \; -1 \leq \mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} \leq 1,
\end{align*}
for $i=1,...,D $ and $0 \leq n_i \leq d$. This has for consequence to reduce computational and memory cost by a factor of roughly $2$. The scalar inequality constraints are enforced using the BM method as described above. In this case, a BM factor $R_k R_k^T$ is degenerate and corresponds to a squared scalar. For instance, the constraint $\mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} \leq 1$ becomes,
$$1-\mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i}  = (t^{(l)}_{i,n_i})^2, \;\; t^{(l)}_{i,n_i} \in \mathbb{R}$$.

\subsubsection{Numerical stability}
The aforementioned constraints are sufficient to ensure that the boundedness of the moments of the product measures within $[-1,1]$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:redundant_constraint}
   -1 \leq   \prod_{i=1}^D \mu^{(l)}_{i,n_i} \leq 1
\end{equation}
for $l=1,...,L$ and $n \in \mathrm{supp}(p)$. Throughout the computations, however, it may occur that such constraints are violated which may in turn result in large values for the product moments and, subsequently, numerical instability. This is particularly dire in higher dimensions where small violations may compound to create large values through multiplication. 

In order to avoid this issue and improve the numerical behavior, our implementation explicitly enforces the (redundant) constraints found in Eq.\eqref{eq:redundant_constraint} by adding them to the formulation found int Eq.\eqref{eq:reformulationcontinuous_BM_general}. 


\subsubsection{Polynomial basis}
Our choice of monomials as a basis for polynomials in Section \ref{sec:notation} was made to facilitate the exposition, although any polynomial basis may have been used. In our implementation, we chose to use a \emph{Chebyshev basis} $\{T_{n_i}(x_i) \}$ (e.g.,\cite{mason2002chebyshev}) since it was found to offer better numerical stability without significantly increasing computational costs. In this context, the only differences are that the 1D moments are defined as,
\begin{equation}
    \mu_{i,n_i} := \int_{-1}^1 \, T_{n_i}(x_i) \, \mathrm{d} \mu_i (x_i)
\end{equation}
and the moment matrices $ \mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i)$ have the form (Hankel + Toeplitz),
\begin{equation}
     [ \mathcal{M}_d(\mu_i) ]_{m_i,n_i} = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \, T_{m_i}(x) \,  T_{n_i} (x_i) \, \mathrm{d} \mu_i(x_i) =  \frac{1}{2} \, \mu_{m_i+n_i} + \frac{1}{2} \, \mu_{\lvert m_i - n_i \rvert}
\end{equation}
which is a consequence of the fact that Chebyshev polynomials satisfy the identity,
\begin{equation}
    T_{m_i}(x_i) \,  T_{n_i}(x_i) = T_{m_i + n_i}(x_i) + T_{\lvert m_i - n_i \rvert} (x_i)
\end{equation}


\subsubsection{Initialization}
Our current implementation uses a simple initialization scheme (no warm start) for the primal and dual variables of the augmented Lagrangian (Eq.\eqref{eq:augmented_lagrangian}) which can be described as follows,
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{primal initialization}: each scalar element of the primal variables,
    \begin{equation*}
    \mu , \, \{R_i^{(l)}, S_i^{(l)}\}
    \end{equation*}
    are initialized randomly using a distribution $U\left ( [-1,1] \right )$ uniform over $[-1,1]$.
    \item \emph{dual initialization}: the dual variables ($\lambda$) are initialized to be zero ($0$).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{L-BFGS and Line Search}
As previously discussed, we use L-BFGS \cite{liu1989limited} to compute descent directions $d$ and solve the primal problem. Next iterates are computed using a line search with backtracking and a Wolfe condition, i.e., the scheme computes the next point so it corresponds to the smallest $k$ such that,
\begin{equation}
    f(x_{\mathrm{current}} + 0.5^k \, d) \leq  f(x_{\mathrm{current}}) + 0.5^k \, \beta \, ( \nabla f(x_{\mathrm{current}}) \cdot d )
\end{equation}
for some parameter $0 < \beta < 1$ (referred to as the Wolfe factor). The order of approximation of the Hessian typically ranges between $20$ and $200$. We also have a mechanism by which the algorithm reverts to a gradient descent direction whenever the line search fails using the direction $d$ computed using L-BFGS.

\subsubsection{Stopping criteria}
We use two (2) types of stopping criteria both for the inner (based on L-BFGS)  and outer (saddle-point) iterations: 1) a criterion based on the size of the relative gradient, and 2) a criterion based on the absolute change in value between the previous and current iterates. We also enforce feasibility, i.e., 
$$ \lvert c_k (x) \rvert < \epsilon_{f},$$
for some stopping parameter $0 < \epsilon_f < 1$ (absolute feasibility) as a part of the stopping criterion for the outer iteration.


\section{Numerical Results}
\label{sec:numerical_results}

This section presents results demonstrating the correctness and performance of our proposed framework and algorithm for optimizing polynomials over the hypercube $[-1,1]^D$. For this purpose, we constructed families of highly non-convex functions with known global optimal value and location (see Figure \ref{fig:sparse} and \ref{fig:dense}). The resulting functions possess characteristics that make global optimization intractable using traditional techniques, namely, non-convexity, exponentially many local minima and exponentially small (in relative volume) basin of attraction as a function of dimension $D$. 

The numerical experiment were carried out on a Dell 1U server with 2x AMD EPYC 7452 32-core (128 system threads) and 256 GB DRAM and were limited to the use of a \emph{single (1) core}. Timing represents wall-time for the whole computations, excluding I/O and the time needed to setup the problem (i.e. pre-computations) which is negligible. Relative error between a computed value $x_{comp}$ and a known value $x_{exact}$ refers to the quantity,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\lvert \lvert x_{comp} - x_{exact} \rvert \rvert_2 }{ 10^{-15} + \lvert \lvert x_{exact} \rvert \rvert_2 }
\end{equation}
where $\lvert \lvert \cdot \rvert \rvert_2$ is the $\ell^2$-norm.


\subsection{Family \#1}
The first family consists in objective polynomials of the form,
\begin{align*}
    f_D (x) &= \frac{1}{D} \, \sum_{i=1}^D T_2(x_i) - \prod_{i=1}^D T_8(x_i) 
    =  \frac{1}{D} \, \sum_{i=1}^D (2 x_i^2-1)  - \prod_{i=1}^D \cos(8 \, \cos^{-1}(x_i))
\end{align*}
Figure \ref{fig:sparse} depicts $f_2(\cdot)$ in two dimensions (i.e., $D=2$). 
