For every fixed dimension $D$, the function $f_D (x)$ possess a unique global minimum at the origin (i.e., $(0,0,...,0)$) with value equal to,
$$f_D^* = f_D(0,0,...,0) = -2.0$$
In addition, each function exhibits more than $3^D$ local minima, and the basin of attraction of the global minimum has volume smaller than $\frac{1}{5^D}$. Together, these characteristics imply that the use of any descent technique is bound to fail. Indeed, any uniformly initialized descent technique for instance, will succeed in finding the global minimum with probability at most $ \frac{1}{5^D}$. In dimension $D=10$, this corresponds to odds of approximately one in $10^{7}$ while in dimension $D=100$, it corresponds to approximately one in $10^{70}$, making the problem intractable for descent techniques in general.

Numerical experiments were performed using the algorithm described in Section \ref{sec:algorithm} together with the parameters shown in Table \ref{tab:param_family_1}. With such a choice of parameters, we expects a relative error smaller than $10^{-2}$. The relative error for the optimal value and the optimal location, as well as the time required before reaching the stopping criterion are shown in Figure \ref{fig:family_1_error} and Figure \ref{fig:family_1_timing} respectively for dimension $D$ from $1$ to $250$.

