We observe a consistent strategy pattern across the three research fields of Chemistry, Physics, and Neuroscience. A larger fraction of all mentees adheres to the \textit{pure follow }strategy compared to elite mentees. The majority of elite mentees adopt the \textit{follow and innovate} strategy, with the orange bar significantly exceeding the blue bar, indicating that elite mentees are more likely to contribute to both shared and new topics. Although both all mentees and elite mentees have the smallest fractions in the \textit{pure innovate} category, elite mentees are slightly less represented here than the general mentee population. Additionally, we examine the complementary cumulative distribution function (CCDF) of $R$, which represents the probability that the degree of a mentee's engagement in new topics is larger than $R$ (Fig. \ref{fig:follow_new}b-d). We observe that across three fields, there is one point where the probabilities of the elite mentee and the general mentee population are equal and the two distributions intersect. This further reveals that, compared to the general mentees, the majority of elite mentees prefer a balanced approach, combining their mentors' topics with new and independent ones (\textit{follow and innovate}, \textcolor{cyan}{Supplementary Fig. S5b}).  
% Overall, these results indicate that elite mentees tend to diversify their research efforts more, while the general mentee population tends to follow their mentors' research areas more closely. 
% \textcolor{red}{Whether marked the value of R for the elite group and general group?}

To further understand how mentees engage with their mentors' research topics, we subdivide the mentors' topics into primary and secondary categories. A topic is designated as "primary" if the proportion of the mentor's papers published in that topic exceeds the median proportion across all their topics; otherwise, it is designated as "secondary". We have validated that mentors are significantly more involved in these primary topics than in secondary topics, suggesting the effectiveness of this dichotomy(\textcolor{cyan}{Supplementary Fig. S6a,e,i}). Note that both primary and secondary topics may encompass multiple distinct topics (\textcolor{cyan}{Supplementary Fig. S6}). We then examine how mentees distribute their research efforts across mentors' primary topics and secondary topics, as well as their new topic communities in the three fields, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:follow_new}e-g. Our analysis reveals that compared to the general mentee population, elite mentees in all three fields exhibit a consistently higher level of diversification under each topic type, particularly in secondary and new topics. This indicates that elite mentees tend to diversify their research efforts more extensively. In Chemistry and Physics (Fig. \ref{fig:follow_new}e and \ref{fig:follow_new}g), elite mentees show a particularly strong tendency to engage in new topics, suggesting a higher propensity for innovation and exploration beyond their mentors' established areas. Additionally, we find that for mentees who have followed their mentors' research topics, both elite and general mentees choose to engage less with their mentors' primary topics rather than secondary topics.

Given the above findings on mentees' engagement in three topic types, we are interested in determining which topic selection types yield the most scientific impact for mentees. To address this, we calculate the average ratio of scientific impact attributed to the mentees' publications within each topic type. Fig. \ref{fig:follow_new}h-j presents the comparative results for both the general mentee population and elite mentees, categorized by their \textit{follow and innovate} and \textit{pure follow} strategies. We can see that both general and elite mentees, regardless of whether they choose a \textit{follow and innovate} or \textit{pure follow} strategy, achieve the lowest impact ratio when engaging in their mentors' primary topics, and this pattern is consistent across all three fields. This is likely in part due to the fact that the saturation and established nature of mentors' primary topics leave limited space for mentees to stand out and make significant contributions. Furthermore, elite researchers who adopt the \textit{follow and innovate} strategy exhibit an interesting balance: they achieve a similar impact ratio on both new topics and their mentor’s secondary topics. This suggests their ability to effectively innovate while still leveraging their mentor's influence in secondary areas. In contrast, the general mentee population shows a higher impact ratio in new topics compared to their mentors' secondary topics. We further analyze the probability of mentees (both general and elite) publishing their highest impactful paper under each topic type (\textcolor{cyan}{Supplementary Fig. S7}), arriving at similar conclusions.
