Here, we investigate whether using a mixture of experts enhances recommendation effectiveness compared to individual experts and baseline models.
% We compare the proposed \proposedcove\ and \proposedcove$_s$\ models with individual experts as well as \hgruforrec.

Results reported in Table~\ref{tab:general_res} consistently show that mixtures of experts outperform the baselines across datasets in terms of MRR, NDCG, and Recall significantly. The AUC metric does not fully reflect recommendation performance, measuring the proportion of pairwise ranking correctly. \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ maintain high AUC results, close to the best reported AUC yet still statistically significant. Similarly, \proposedcove$_s$ also achieves the best performance across datasets. Among the selected experts, \gruforrec\ remains the best-performing model overall, showing its robustness in the sequential recommendation task.
% After incorporating \gruforrec\ as one expert, both \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ further leverage the strength of other experts in modeling long-term preferences and improving the recommendation effectiveness.
The session-aware baseline \hgruforrec\ does not outperform \gruforrec, which has also been reported in some benchmark~\cite{latifi2021session}.
% Although FPMC achieves the best AUC in Cosmetics dataset, it recommendation performance in terms of MRR, NDCG, and Recall are low generally.

FPMC itself can be interpreted as a combination of Matrix Factorization (MF) and Factorization Markov Chains (FMC), which is similar to a composition of one long-term (MF) and one short-term (FMC) expert. However, each component contributes equally towards final optimization objective.
% We further investigate this phenomenon in the subsequent section (\autoref{subsec:uniform-gate-performance}).


% For three individual experts, BPR, FPMC, and GRU4Rec, we observe that GRU4Rec consistently outperforms the other two experts across all datasets. This result is expected, as GRU4Rec is specifically designed for session-based recommendation and can capture sequential user behavior more effectively. However, when comparing the proposed \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ models with individual experts, we find that \proposedcove$_s$ and \proposedcove$_h$ consistently outperform other models across all datasets in most of the metrics, except AUC in Cosmetics dataset, where \proposedcove$_h$ is the second best. Between \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$, there is no clear winner, as \proposedcove$_s$ is better in Diginetica, \proposedcove$_h$ is better in Cosmetics, and both are comparable in RetailRocket. This result suggests that the proposed mixture models can effectively leverage the strengths of different experts to improve recommendation performance.

