{\bf Gating effectiveness.}
To further emphasize the contribution of the proposed gating mechanism, we evaluate the performance of both variants of \proposedcove\ without the learnable weights, the gate values are always uniform values, i.e., $g(x)_i=\frac{1}{K}$, where $K$ is the number of experts, which assuming all experts contribute equally towards the overall optimization objective. Table~\ref{tab:uniform_gate} reports the ranking performance of \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ with and without the learnable weights from the proposed gating mechanism. Without the flexibility of controlling how much each expert contributes, we observed a degradation in the recommendation performance for both \proposedcove$_s$ and \proposedcove$_h$ in term of ranking performance. \proposedcove$_h$ without the learnable gating achieves a bit higher AUC measure in both datasets, however, it is not statistically significant different from \proposedcove$_h$ with learnable gating.  Thus, balancing the contribution among variant experts is not a trivial task.


% We compare the performance of the proposed \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ models but the gate always returns uniform values (i.e., 1/K for each expert) versus GRU4Rec, which is the best-performing individual expert. The uniform gate is a trivial task, as it does not require the model to learn the importance of each expert. This can be seen as an ensemble method with all experts contributing equally to the final prediction. The learnable gate, on the other hand, allows the model to determine the importance of each expert based on the input data. The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:uniform_gate}.

% Uniform gate \proposedcove$_s$ and move cannot outperform GRU4Rec, the best individual expert, while Table~\ref{tab:general_res} shows learnable gate \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ consistently outperform GRU4Rec.

% conclusion: balancing long and short-term preferences is not a trivial task.


% \subsection{RQ3: Is combining multiple models for session-aware recommendation trivial?}
% \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$ with gating perform better than non-gate (uniform weights) mixtures

{\bf Same-Type Experts vs. Varying-Type Experts.}
The results from~\autoref{sec:short-vs-long-term-preferences} indicate the presence of both short-term and long-term preferences within the session-aware recommendation context. We categorize the compositions of experts into two sub-categories: same-type experts and varying-type experts. The same-type expert mixture includes only short-term (or long-term) experts, while the varying-type expert mixture comprises both short-term and long-term experts. We further explore different compositions of variant experts, including combinations of 2, 3, and 4 experts with varying types (which include both short-term and long-term experts). The results for these compositions on the Diginetica dataset are presented in ~\autoref{tab:diginetica-varying-experts}, while compositions for the RetailRocket dataset are shown in ~\autoref{tab:retail-varying-experts}.

As demonstrated in ~\autoref{tab:diginetica-varying-experts}, compositions of the same-type experts for \proposedcove$_h$ result in a performance degradation compared to individual experts, whereas compositions of varying types lead to significant improvements.
For \proposedcove$_s$, it appears that it is easier to achieve better results compared to individual experts. The key factor is identifying the optimal expert composition; in this case, the selection of the optimal composition depends on the respecting metric.

The results in ~\autoref{tab:retail-varying-experts} show a similar pattern, where the best compositions consistently include cross-type experts (\gruforrec+\bpr) for both \proposedcove$_h$ and \proposedcove$_s$.



