\section{Methodology} \label{sec:methodology}
In this study, we explore state-of-the-art methods as baselines to evaluate the FiboSB dataset. \sm{Most} 6D pose estimation methods use a two-stage pipeline: (1) object detection, which provides 2D bounding boxes for predicted objects, and (2) 6D pose estimation, which predicts the objects' 3D translations and orientations based on the interest regions from the first stage. %Section \ref{sec:object_detection} and \ref{sec:6d_pose_estimation} describe more details of each stage and corresponding evaluation metrics.

% \label{sec:object_detection}
% Object detection allows us to identify object candidates using 2D bounding boxes and corresponding class indices within an image. The candidates are filtered based on a threshold to remove the overlapped bounding boxes for the same object. Faster R-CNN \cj{\cite{ren2016faster}}, YOLO \cj{\cite{terven2023comprehensive}}, and RetinaNet \cj{\cite{ross2017focal}} are well-established architectures to target fine-grained detections, and the technique facilitates 6D pose estimation methods to find precise translations and orientations.
\textbf{Object Detection metrics:} We evaluated the initial stage of the state-of-the-art 6D pose estimation approaches, which is the object detection modules, by employing the $mAP_{50}$ metric (mean of Average Precision (AP) at Intersection of Union (IoU) threshold of 0.5) \cjj{\cite{everingham2015pascal,everingham2010pascal}. The values of $mAP_{50}$ range between 0\% to 100\%, and a higher value represents better performance.}
