\section{Introduction} \label{Introduction}

The success of Large Language Models (LLMs) can be attributed to their ability to leverage large amounts of public data and intricate parameter networks~\cite{kaplan2020scaling}. By training on extensive corpora, LLMs can capture statistical patterns in language, semantic structures, and subtle contextual relationships. However, private data holds a significant advantage over public datasets in terms of both quality and utibility. This is because private datasets often contain more granular and specific insights about individuals or organizations, typically collected through more controlled and precise methodologies~\cite{yang2024federated}. Additionally, private data tend to be more timely, targeted, and unique, thus providing models with semantic contexts and environmental settings that more closely mirror real-world scenarios~\cite{ye2024openfedllm}. Therefore, effectively incorporating private data will become one of the critical factors in further advancing the performance of LLMs.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{images/FL-LLaMA.pdf}
    \caption{
    Model architecture of FedBert and FL-LLaMA. 
   }
    \label{fig:compare}
\end{figure}

The distributed nature of private data across various silos and the significant computational demands of LLMs present substantial challenges for their deployment in federated environments. Private data is often isolated in local silos, such as mobile devices~\cite{ghimire2022recent}, enterprises servers~\cite{huang2024federated} or medical institutions~\cite{qi2024model,yuan2024decentralized}, as centralized storage and processing of such data may raise privacy concerns and regulatory risks~\cite{chen2024federated}. Federated Learning (FL) offers a promising solution by enabling local training on user devices and only sending model parameters for aggregation, but traditional FL frameworks, such as FedAvg~\cite{stremmel2021pretraining} and FedProx~\cite{li2020federated}, are infeasible for LLMs training scenarios. LLMs typically have billions of parameters, and their training process requires extensive computational resources, placing substantial demands on device memory and network bandwidth. This makes training the full model on ordinary client devices practically infeasible. Additionally, the communication overhead and heterogeneity inherent in LLMs training become even more pronounced within federated environments, making it challenging for models to efficiently converge or maintain generalization capabilities. As a result, how to effectively deploy LLMs within FL frameworks while ensuring privacy and scalability remains an open and cutting-edge research topic.


As an alternative method, transformer-based split learning methods, named FedBERT~\cite{tian2022fedbert}, have been proposed to adapt LLMs, offloading most model parameters reside to the server while retaining only the embedding and output layers on client to ensure privacy. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:compare}(a), FedBERT divides Transformers into three parts, i.e., embedding layers and output layers on client, while main Transformer layers on server. The forward propagation(FP) path is client-to-server-to-client. After client calculates loss, gradients backpropagate(BP) from client-to-server-to-client. The core idea of FedBERT is to divide the calculation into two parts: the client devices is responsible for initial feature extraction from raw data and calculate final loss, while the server handles deeper inference and learning. Although FedBERT provides a potentially viable solution for resource-constrained clients scenarios, it still faces several limitations: \textbf{1) Privacy Leakage:} during forward and backward propagation, activation values and gradients from the embedding layer are transmitted, which can be exploited to reconstruct original input data through beam search~\cite{gupta2022recovering} or reverse engineering~\cite{asnani2023reverse}. \textbf{2) Communication Cost:} LLMs are auto-regressive, requiring sequential token generation based on prior tokens. In federated split learning, this necessitates a full forward and backward pass with client-server communication for each token, greatly increasing communication rounds and bandwidth usage. \textbf{3) Lack of Adaptability: } Fixed partition points lack flexibility to downstream tasks and hardware constraints, as different devices vary in their capacity to handle workloads and parameter sizes. 

In this paper, we introduce FL-LLaMA, a secure, efficient, and adaptive federated split framework based on LLaMA2, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:compare}(b). To \textbf{Enable Secure Propagation}, we partition the transformers of LLaMA2 into three parts: input and output blocks on clients, while the remaining large parameters on server. During training, clients perform forward propagation to generate hidden states, add Gaussian noise for them, and send these noised states to the server for further processing. Through both gradients analysis and experimental verification, this noise ensures privacy and security during subsequent training and inference stages. To \textbf{Reduce Communication Cost}, we employ client-batch and server-hierarchical strategies to achieve parallel training: the former batches data from different clients, while the latter leverages concurrent sub-server to enhance training efficiency. Durning inference, we introduce the attention-mask compressio and collaborative KV cache mechanisms, minimizing overhead while preserving the integrity of autoregressive inference. By synchronously maintaining cache states on both client and server, the originally cross-device transmition is transformed into dynamically generated local data, reducing communication from megabytes(MB) to bytes(B). To \textbf{Achieve Adaptive Partition}, we design a dynamic partition strategy allowing users to flexibly adjust the number of input and output blocks according to specific downstream tasks and available computational resources. This adaptability enables optimal balance between performance and efficiency.


Experiments on NLU, summarization, and conversational QA tasks show that FL-LLaMA maintains performance comparable to centralized LLaMA2, and achieving up to 2x training and 8× inference speedups. Through inference acceleration, the average token generation time of FL-LLaMA has been reduced by 87.6\%, and the speed gain will further increase with the length of context.
\footnote{Our code is available at: \href{https://github.com/TAP-LLM/SplitFedLLM}{https://github.com/TAP-LLM/SplitFedLLM.} }

The innovations in this paper are as follows:
