This paper is an extension of the work presented in ~\cite{zheng2024safely}. There are five major differences from the original version: (i) the base model and fine-tuning method have been updated, from p-tuning v2 on ChatGLM in the original version to LoRA on LLaMA2, demonstrating the generalizability of our federated split framework; (ii) Gaussian noise is introduced during the forward pass of split learning to further enhance the security of the federated split LLMs; (iii) the use of attention-mask compression and collaborative KV cache mechanism significantly improves inference efficiency in the federated split setting; (iv) dynamic partitioning is supported, allowing users to customize split points via configuration files, thus improving adaptability; (v) the experimental evaluation has been extended by assessing FL-LLaMA on new summarization and conversational QA tasks, showcasing its versatility.

