\section{Related work} \label{sec:relatedwork}
\subsection{Fedrated Learning for LLMs}

Federated Learning (FL) has emerged as a promising paradigm for training language model in a decentralized manner while preserving user privacy and ensuring data security. FedAvg~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} is one of the earliest and most widely adopted optimization algorithms, which has been successfully applied in multiple language modeling tasks~\cite{hard2018federated, chen2019federated, stremmel2021pretraining}. In this approach, each client locally trains a model on its private data and sends the updates to a central server, which performs a weighted aggregation to produce a new global model. To reduce the number of local training rounds and accelerate convergence, Stremmel et al.~\cite{stremmel2021pretraining} propose initializing the global model with a pretrained language model before applying FedAvg, which has proven effective in speeding up training. Ji et al.~\cite{ji2019learning} introduce FedAtt, which incorporates a hierarchical soft attention mechanism during aggregation to adaptively weighs each client's model parameters based on their contribution, improving both generalization and local adaptation.

In terms of LLMs, traditional FL frameworks face growing challenges due to increased communication and computational costs. To address the issue of massive model sizes, some federated LLMs framework ~\cite{fan2023fate, kuang2024federatedscope, ye2024openfedllm} has been proposed to leverage instructions tuning, Adapters and LoRA for local fine-tuning, which require updating only a small subset of parameters and significantly reducing communication costs while maintaining performance. But these methods still require the transmission of parameter quantities far exceeding the system's carrying capacity. To further minimize communication overhead, gradient compression~\cite{wu2024cg, shu2024ferret} and quantization~\cite{jianhao2024promoting} mechanisms have been explored, which effectively reduce the volume of data exchanged, 
%between clients and servers, 
making them well-suited for federated fine-tuning of LLMs. Liu et al.~\cite{liu2024fisher} propose structural pruning approaches that update only selected parameter layers of LLMs based on Fisher Information. By narrowing the update scope, these methods effectively reduce the size of transmitted models without sacrificing overall performance. 

Besides knowledge distillation methods between large and small language models~\cite{wu2024fedbiot, fan2024fedcollm, fan2025fedmkt} are proposed to avoid the high communication cost of transferring LLMs parameters directly. It utilize the smaller proxy models acting as intermediaries, facilitating knowledge transfer between clients and the server with substantially lower communication. Additionally, client heterogeneity remains a central challenge in FL, due to variations in computational capacity, data distributions, and task types across clients~\cite{zhang2024towards}. Bai et al.~\cite{bai2024federated} and Wang et al.~\cite{wang2024flora} introduce LoRA-based stacking strategies to balance the differences in client capabilities. They help ensure that models from clients with varying ranks can still be aggregated effectively, improving training stability and fairness.

While recent works of federated LLMs explores efficient fine-tuning and parameter reduction methods, resource-constrained clients still struggle with independent fine-tuning, especially with added compression overhead. Therefore, split learning has emerged as a viable alternative for such scenarios. 


\subsection{Split Learning for LLMs}
Split learning~\cite{thapa2022splitfed} is gaining traction as a decentralized and collaborative training paradigm tailored for environments with limited computational resources~\cite{abedi2024fedsl,abuadbba2020can,rahman2020internet,matsubara2021neural}, particularly suited for scenarios involving mobile platforms or lightweight clients that lack access to GPUs. The core idea involves partitioning a deep neural network into separate sub-network, with each sub-network assigned to a different device for distributed processing.

To address the training of sequential data in LLMs, FedBERT~\cite{tian2022fedbert} introduces an innovative federated learning framework designed to pre-train language models in a split architecture, addressing the challenge of limited computational capacity on client devices. In this approach, the BERT model is divided into three core components: the Embedding layer, the Transformer layer, and the Output layer. This separation allows the heavy Transformer layer to be trained on a centralized, high-performance server, while the lighter Embedding and Output layers are handled locally by client devices. Although this design enables more efficient distribution of training workloads, it comes with drawbacks, such as increased communication overhead, fixed partition points and susceptibility to privacy threats like embedding gradient attacks.

Recently, Su et al.~\cite{su2024titanic} extend the scope of split learning by exploring scenarios with heterogeneous resource configurations, particularly considering a case where the server lacks sufficient computational capacity. In their approach, the LLM is automatically divided into $k$ partitions, each assigned to one of $k$ clients, while the server merely performs aggregation. Although this strategy alleviates server-side computation, it considerably increases the overall training and inference time for LLMs. Furthermore, the repeated transmission of hidden states increases the risk of single points of failure and introduces potential security vulnerabilities.

Our work diverges from these studies by targeting a scenario with a powerful server and resource-constrained clients holding private data. We prioritize enhancing both training/inference efficiency, adaptive partition points and data privacy for distributed LLMs through optimized algorithmic designs and communication strategies.

