\subsection{Attacks and Defenses}

In federated learning, although it allows multiple clients to jointly fine-tune models without revealing raw data, there remains a risk of embedding-based reconstruction attacks, LoRA-based gradient inversion attacks and split learning-based data reconstructing attacks. 

Attacks from embedding gradients have proven highly effective in reconstructing users’ private data. Gupta et al.~\cite{gupta2022recovering} demonstrated that by identifying non-zero elements in the embedding gradients, an adversary can infer which tokens were used by the client,  using beam search to reconstruct private data. To mitigate such attacks, they proposed freezing the embedding layer during training. Zhu et al.~\cite{zhu2019deep} briefly discussed defensive strategies such as injecting differential privacy noise~\cite{dwork2006calibrating, wei2020federated}  or applying gradient clipping by zeroing out small gradients. Huang et al. \cite{huang2020texthide} introduced a MixUp-based data augmentation method on the [CLS] token in BERT, while Yaldiz et al.~\cite{yaldiz2023secure} suggested applying cosine similarity checks on client-uploaded weights to detect and filter out malicious clients. But these defenses often degrade the model's accuracy~\cite{yu2024differentially, li2021large}.

In addition to embedding-based reconstruction attacks, Federated LLMs are also vulnerable to gradient inversion attacks via LoRA, due to frequent parameter sharing across clients~\cite{jalalirad2019simple, thakkar2020understanding}. To address this, Liu et al.~\cite{liu2025differentially} propose DP-LoRA, which adds Gaussian noise to LoRA updates to ensure differential privacy~\cite{dwork2006calibration}~\cite{wei2020federated}. However, the combination of heterogeneous data, DP noise, and LoRA's sensitivity to hyperparameters can lead to instability and performance loss. To overcome this, Sun et al.~\cite{sun2024improving} introduced FFA-LoRA, which initializes the non-zero entries of the LoRA \( A \) matrix from a Gaussian distribution and keeps matrix \( A \) frozen while only fine-tuning the zero-initialized LoRA \( B \) matrix, thus preventing the injected noise under differential privacy from being amplified. However, exposure of intermediate activations and gradients in distributed training still enables potential data reconstruction, prompting increased focus on secure split learning methods.

In split learning, Ege et al.~\cite{erdougan2022unsplit} propose inferring labels by minimizing gradient differences between a pseudo-model and the actual model, achieving strong performance in classification tasks. However, this approach is impractical for LLMs due to the high computational cost of handling vocabularies exceeding 100,000 tokens, making real-time federated training infeasible. In image recognition, Dario et al.~\cite{pasquini2021unleashing} show that a malicious server could reconstruct client data by exploiting hidden states via a Feature Space Hijacking Attack (FSHA). However, FSHA relies on access to a shadow dataset from the same domain and task—an unrealistic assumption in privacy-preserving settings, where servers lack access to such data.


Building on prior attacks, we explore a more severe threat model where a malicious client colludes with an honest-but-curious server by sharing its data, enabling the server to train an attack model that reconstructs plaintext from other clients using hidden states. Importantly, the server does not alter model outputs or gradients. 


