\section{Framework} \label{sec:framework}
In this section, we present a detailed description of the FL-LLaMA framework, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:FL-LLaMA-Framework}, which comprises five components: split learning, privacy preservation, parallel training, inference acceleration and adaptive partitioning. First, we adopt split learning to divide the LLaMA into three parts: the local client stores the first ${p}$ blocks and the last ${q}$ blocks, while the remaining ${k}$ blocks, comprising the majority of the model parameters, are hosted on the server. Next, we design a Gaussian noise injection mechanism applied to the forward-pass hidden states to ensure data privacy during transmission between the client and server. Third, we enhance the training efficiency of split learning through a client-batch and server-hierarchical parallelisms. Fourth, we accelerate inference by leveraging the attention mask compression and collaborative KV cache mechanisms. Finally, the framework allows clients to adaptively adjust the split configuration based on their privacy preferences and available computational resources.


\subsection{Split Learning}
Suppossing that the LLMs comprises $N$ transformer blocks, the proposed FL-LLaMA framework partitions the LLMs into three segments: Part I contains the embedding layer and the first $p$ transformer blocks, denoted as $Blocks_A$; Part II includes the middle $k$ transformer blocks, denoted as $Blocks_B$; Part III consists of the last $q$ transformer blocks along with the output layer, denoted as $Blocks_C$, where $p+k+q=N$. For generative tasks, the model predicts the next token autoregressively, with data labels closely linked to the input text sequences. To safeguard client data privacy, the FL-LLaMA framework places Part I $Blocks_A$ and Part III $Blocks_C$ on the client side, while Part II $Blocks_B$ resides on the server side, thereby preventing direct exposure of client data. Such an architecture optimizes GPU memory allocation, as the server handles most of the model's GPU memory burden, significantly reducing memory pressure on the client. 

The architecture diagram of the proposed model is shown on the left of Fig.~\ref{fig:FL-LLaMA-Framework}.
 %这里后面统一用的 Fig. 2 而不是 Figure 2 得统一一下
Taking a single-client distributed training scenario as an example, at the beginning of training, the client utilizes local data to perform forward propagation through $Blocks_A$, resulting in hidden states \(h_{A}\):
\begin{equation}
h_{A} = f_{1 \sim p} \circ f_{\text{embed}}(\mathbf{x}),
\end{equation}
where \(f_{\text{embed}}(\cdot)\) denotes the embedding layer of the LLMs, and \(f_{1 \sim p}(\cdot)\) represents the sequential forward propagation through transformer block 1 to block $p$ at the client side. 

After obtaining \(h_{A}\), the client transmits it together with the position index and the attention mask matrix $A_{mask}\in [0,-\infty]^{b \times seq\_len \times seq\_len}$ to the server, where $b$ is the batch size and $seq\_len$ is the maximum context length in LLMs. Subsequently, $Blocks_B$ on the server side takes the received hidden states \(h_{A}\) and attention mask $A_{mask}$ as inputs, performs computationally intensive tasks, and returns the resulting hidden states \(h_{B}\) back to the client:
\begin{equation}
h_{B} = f_{p+1 \sim p+k}(h_{A},A_{mask}),
\end{equation}
where \(f_{p+1 \sim p+k}(\cdot)\) denotes sequential forward propagation through transformer blocks \(p+1\) to \(p+k\) on the server side.

Finally, $Blocks_C$ on the client-side performs computations to obtain the logits \(\mathbf{z}\), computes the loss with local data, and initiates gradient backpropagation. These steps are defined by the following equations:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{z} = f_{p+k+1 \sim N}(h_{B},A_{mask}), 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{F}_{loss}(f_{out}(\mathbf{z}), \mathbf{y}),
\end{equation}
where \(f_{p+k+1 \sim N}(\cdot)\) denotes sequential forward propagation through transformer blocks \(p+k+1\) to \(N\) on the client side, \(f_{out}(\cdot)\) is the output layer of the model, $\mathbf{y}$ is the targeting sentences and \(\mathcal{F}_{loss}(\cdot)\) denotes the cross-entropy loss function. 

After computing the loss, the client performs gradient backpropagation locally to update $Blocks_C$, then transmits the gradient of the hidden states \(h_{B}\) back to the server. The server executes gradient backpropagation on $Blocks_B$, updates its parameters, and subsequently transmits the gradients of hidden states \(h_{A}\) back to the client. Lastly, $Blocks_A$ performs gradient backpropagation, updates the model and embedding parameters, and completes a sequential training round. 


\subsection{Privacy Preservation}
In distributed training of LLMs, the split learning paradigm partitions the entire model into multiple sub-models deployed across different physical nodes. As a result, the hidden states and gradients must be transferred across nodes via the network. Unlike traditional federated learning, which only shares gradients, this approach exposes mid-layer activations and their gradients, greatly increasing the risk of adversaries reverse-engineering user data and challenging the data protection framework of federated split LLMs.
