\subsection{Inference Accelaration}
The inference time of LLMs are critically important for both enterprises and end-users. Low-latency inference minimizes user wait times, allowing for efficient and fast handling of complex tasks. Distributed architectures partition tensor operations, traditionally executed within GPUs and directly interacting with high-bandwidth memory (HBM), into separate computational units across multiple physical devices. Consequently, intermediate data must be transferred over networks, posing substantial latency challenges for the inference of LLMs. To enhance the efficiency and usability of distributed LLMs, our proposed FL-LLaMA framework incorporates attention mask compression and collaborative KV cache mechanisms to minimize redundant data transmission. 
%Low-latency training enables enterprises to rapidly iterate over various training strategies, thereby significantly reducing training costs. 
\subsubsection{Attention Mask Compression}
FL-LLaMA transmits only the minimal information necessary for effective model training and inference. One significant improvement arises from reducing redundancy in attention mask transmissions. In masked self-attention mechanisms, the attention mask serves two primary purposes: (1) preventing decoders from prematurely accessing subsequent tokens during prediction, and (2) ensuring that padding tokens within input data do not interfere with attention computations involving valid tokens. Notably, the generation of attention masks across transformer blocks remains consistent throughout each training iteration and depends solely on dataset padding. Consequently, rather than transmitting the entire attention mask tensors $A_{mask}\in [0,-\infty]^{b \times seq\_len \times seq\_len}$, which incurs substantial bandwidth latency, the FL-LLaMA framework only sends essential metadata ($seq\_len$, $pad\_len$) indicating the locations of padding tokens versus valid tokens from the client to the server, thus significantly decreasing communication overhead.


\subsubsection{Collaborative KV Cache Mechanism}
Since the model autoregressively generates the next token based on the tokens already generated, the speed of generating new tokens slows down as the input sequence length becomes excessively long (e.g., greater than 1000), due to the increasing number of K and V calculations. The application of key-value cache (KV cache) techniques in distributed inference further enhances inference speed. Generative LLMs produce outputs autoregressively, meaning that each generated token becomes part of the input sequence used to generate subsequent tokens. As user queries grow longer, repeatedly transmitting the complete context—comprising the initial query and previously generated tokens—from client to server can drastically increase inference latency. To mitigate this issue, the collaborative KV cache mechanism locally stores previously computed key and value states from the attention layers. Since these states remain unchanged throughout token generation, newly generated tokens reuse cached states without requiring recomputation. Building upon this insight, FL-LLaMA caches the KV states locally at both the client and server sides, thereby restricting communication to the hidden state of the single newly-generated token at each inference step. This approach effectively minimizes data transmission, substantially improving inference efficiency.

\subsection{Adaptive Partition}
%FL-GLM~\cite{zheng2024safely} supports splitting the model only at the first and last blocks on the client side, while all other parameters are maintained on the server. 
Ege et al.\cite{erdougan2022unsplit} and Dario et al.\cite{pasquini2021unleashing} have pointed out that deeper model splits make it significantly harder for an attacker to recover information from the hidden states, as these data are produced through more complex computations. Furthermore, in real-world scenarios where the number of clients is large, the server may encounter computational resource bottlenecks, whereas clients are likely to have comparatively abundant computing resources.

To flexibly accommodate specific task requirements and hardware constraints, we propose an adaptive splitting strategy that enables users to adjust the number of model input and output blocks in accordance with the demands of downstream tasks and available computational resources, thereby achieving an optimal balance between performance and efficiency. Based on this strategy, we explore the performance of downstream tasks when the client holds the first blocks (with Blocks\_A=1,2,3) and the last blocks (with Blocks\_C=1,2,3) of the model in Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptive}. The results indicate that the depth of the model split does not significantly impact performance on downstream tasks, while affording a more flexible allocation of computational loads between the server and the clients. Furthermore, it corroborates the efficacy of our adaptive splitting strategy and delineates a refined configuration paradigm for deploying LLMs in federated learning environments.

\section{Experiments} \label{sec:experiment}
In order to demonstrate the performance of LLaMA within the federated split framework (FL-LLaMA), we conduct experiments using the same benchmarks as those used in LLaMA2-7B model~\cite{touvron2023LLaMA}. Developed by Meta AI, the LLaMA2-7B model serves as a general-purpose language model with strong performance in natural language processing tasks, particularly when fine-tuned for specific domains. We employ this architecture to investigate whether FL-LLaMA can maintain comparable task performance to conventional centralized training, while addressing high delays and data privacy concerns through decentralized learning. 


