\subsection{Experimental Settings}
In this section, we introduce some empirical settings, including datasets, evaluation metrics, baselines and parameter settings for FL-LLaMA.

\subsubsection{Dataset}
To evaluate model generalization across diverse NLP tasks, our experiments incorporates three distinct tasks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The SuperGLUE benchmark~\cite{wang2020superglue} is designed for evaluating natural language understanding (NLU) tasks with eight distinct tasks, which span a broad range of linguistic challenges, from common sense reasoning to fine-grained semantic interpretation. Table~\ref{tb:superglueTask} illustrates examples of cloze-style questions and corresponding answers derived from each task. (1)\textbf{ReCoRD} tests reading comprehension with common sense reasoning by resolving ambiguous entities in passages. (2)\textbf{COPA} assesses causal reasoning by choosing the more likely cause or effect for a given premise. (3)\textbf{WSC} focuses on pronoun disambiguation requiring deep contextual understanding. (4)\textbf{RTE} evaluates textual entailment by determining if one sentence follows, contradicts, or is unrelated to another. (5)\textbf{BoolQ} involves yes/no questions answered using evidence from context passages. (6)\textbf{WiC} tests word sense disambiguation by checking if a word has the same meaning in two sentences. (7)\textbf{CB} deals with entailment in complex sentences with nuanced reasoning. (8)\textbf{MultiRC} features multi-answer questions based on multi-sentence contexts, requiring integration of dispersed information. To unify these tasks under a single modeling framework, we adopt the formulation used in GLM~\cite{glm}, representing each instance as a blank infilling problem. Given a labeled pair \((x, y)\), the input \(x\) is converted into a masked cloze question \(q(x)\) using a placeholder token \([M]\), while the output \(y\) serves as the target answer \(a(y)\).

    \item The CoQA dataset~\cite{reddy2019coqa} is designed for the task of conversational question answering(QA), focusing on evaluating contextual coherence in multi-turn dialogues. It includes over 8,000 dialogues and more than 127,000 questions drawn from seven diverse domains. Answers are provided in free-form text, with nearly half of the questions requiring coreference resolution (e.g., pronoun disambiguation) and pragmatic reasoning. This setup closely mirrors real-world conversational settings, aiming to assess a model's ability to understand text passages and respond to a series of interrelated questions.
    \item The XSum dataset~\cite{narayan2018don} is designed for the task of abstractive summarization, specifically evaluating a model's ability to perform extreme compression of salient information from news articles. It consists of BBC news articles paired with manually written summaries, covering a broad range of topics. A key challenge of XSum lies in its requirement for the model to generate a single-sentence summary for each article, placing significant demands on the model’s ability to abstract and condense information effectively.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Metrics} 
Since NLU tasks are reformulated as blank infilling problems, model performance is assessed based on the probability assigned to the ground-truth answer \( a(y) \). For datasets such as RTE, BoolQ, WiC, CB, and MultiRC where the correct answer is often a single token, the evaluation score is derived from the logit corresponding to that token. Specifically, we define the probability as follows:
\[
p(y|x)=\frac{p(a(y)|q(x))}{ \sum_{y^{'} \in Y} p(a(y^{'})|q(x))},
\]
where \( Y \) represents the set of possible ground-truth labels.

In contrast, datasets like ReCoRD, COPA, and WSC require multi-token answers. In these cases, we evaluate model performance by summing the log-probabilities of each token in the predicted answer, computed as:
\[
s(y|x)=\sum_{t=1}^{|L_{y}| }\text{log} \text{P}(y_{t }|y_{1}...y_{t-1},x;\theta ).
\]

For conversational QA tasks, we utilize two metrics: Exact Match (EM) and F1 score to compare the model performance, which evaluate the accuracy of predicted answers against the ground truth~\cite{yatskar2019qualitative, you2022end}. Exact Match (EM) measures the percentage of predictions that match the ground truth answers exactly, accounting for variations in punctuation and case. The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, providing a more nuanced view of performance by rewarding partial overlaps between the predicted and reference answers. 

For summarization tasks, we adopt standard ROUGE metrics~\cite{liu2021topic,chen2020multi,fang2022spoken}, i.e., ROUGE-1 and ROUGE-2, which are widely used to measure content overlap between generated summaries and reference texts. ROUGE-1 evaluates the unigram overlap, while ROUGE-2 measures the bigram overlap, providing insights into both content coverage and fluency. 

\subsubsection{Baselines} 
We apply FL-LLaMA to LLaMA2-7B model~\cite{touvron2023LLaMA}, who is an open-source pre-trained language model with 7 billion parameters. For a fair comparison with LLaMA2-7B, we use 5 baselines, including $\text{T5}_{\text{large}}$~\cite{raffel2020exploring}, $\text{BART}_{\text{Large}}$~\cite{lewis2020bart}, $\text{GLM}_{\text{RoBERTa}}$~\cite{glm}, $\text{ChatGLM-6B}$~\cite{glm2024chatglm} and LLaMA2-7B~\cite{touvron2023LLaMA}.



