\subsubsection{Parallel Training} \label{sec:Parallel Training}

For communication cost analysis, we randomly selected 1,000 samples from the ReCoRD dataset. In our experimental setup, all training used a batch size of 1 and adopted FP16 training to improve computational efficiency. For LoRA, the decomposition rank $r$ is set to 8, and the scaling factor $\alpha$ is configured to 16, focusing specifically on the attention modules during fine-tuning. Additionally, the model is partitioned with Blocks\_A=1 and Blocks\_C=1. Each experiment is repeated five times, and we report the mean and standard deviation of the total communication time, as summarized in Tab. \ref{tab:training_time_comparison}. The results show that sequential training takes the longest time, followed by client-batch training, and server hierarchical takes the shortest time. However, when the server can accommodate a larger batch size, increasing the number of clients in client-batch training effectively increases the batch size and thus reduces overall training time. 

