\subsubsection{Effects of Noise Scales} \label{sec:Noise injection}
% 这里不会有人让我们用其他噪声类型例如laplace噪声再做一下吧？
Since noise injection into the hidden states during forward propagation inevitably impacts the performance of distributed LLMs, we conducted extensive experiments to investigate how varying levels of noise affect model performance. 
%The detailed experimental results can be found in Section \ref{sec:Noise injection}.
The results demonstrate that introducing appropriate levels of noise leads to only a slight decrease in the performance of distributed LLMs, while significantly enhancing their security.

%主要探究不同噪声不同分割点对下游任务的影响
The magnitude of noise injection significantly influences the stability and convergence speed of model training. Given that noise is directly injected into the hidden state \(h_A\), the relative magnitude between the noise and \(h_A\) naturally affects the model's performance. To quantify this effect, we first fine-tuned the FL-LLaMA model on the MultiRC \cite{khashabi2018looking} with a batch size of 2 and a learning rate of \(1 \times 10^{-5}\), analyzing the average absolute magnitude of all elements within hidden state \(h_A\). According to Fig. \ref{fig:Avg magnitude of hidden state h_A}, the average magnitude of the hidden state \(h_A\) is approximately 0.02 ($num_{Blocks_A}$=1). The average magnitude of hidden state $h_A$ increases with the number of blocks in $Blocks_A$. Based on this observation, we subsequently injected noise at various scales around this value during training to investigate how the noise magnitude affects both the convergence speed and downstream task performance. 
%这里的实验参数应该是不同分割点的实验参数
Training is conducted for 5,000 steps with FP16 precision. We used LoRA with a rank of 8 and a scaling factor $\alpha = 16$. The learning rate is 1e-5, and the batch size is 2. 

The corresponding experimental results are presented in Fig. \ref{fig:Loss_for_Blocks_A_1}-\ref{fig:Loss_for_Blocks_A_3} and Tab. \ref{tab:performance_modelA_noise}. 
Fig. \ref{fig:Loss_for_Blocks_A_1}-\ref{fig:Loss_for_Blocks_A_3} demonstrate that when the number of blocks in $Blocks_A$ and $Blocks_C$ are held constant, the convergence speed of FL-LLaMA decreases as the magnitude of injected noise increases. When the average absolute value of the noise is small (e.g., below 0.02), the loss curve remains close to that of the noise-free baseline. A horizontal comparison reveals that, under the same noise level, increasing the number of blcoks in $Blocks_A$ consistently accelerates convergence. 

Tab. \ref{tab:performance_modelA_noise} indicates that under fixed noise levels, increasing the number of blocks in $Blocks_A$ consistently improves downstream performance in terms of both F1 and EM scores. Notably, when the noise amplitude reaches 0.1, only the single-block configuration of $Blocks_A$ shows a significant drop in downstream performance, whereas the three-block version of $Blocks_A$ demonstrates greater robustness. This can be explained by the average magnitude shown in Fig. \ref{fig:Avg magnitude of hidden state h_A}: as the depth of $Blocks_A$ increases, the ratio between the average absolute value of the hidden state $h_A$ and the noise amplitude also increases, thereby improving the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR). This enhanced SNR is precisely what contributes to the better convergence and stronger robustness observed with a larger number of $Blocks_A$.                   


