\subsubsection{Model Reverse Attack}\label{sec:Model Reverse Attack}
To investigate the impact of split point selection and noise scale on the security of FL-LLaMA, we evaluate the attack performance of the adversarial model under varying configurations of $Blocks_A$ depth (Blocks\_A = 1, 2, 3) and noise (scale = 0, 0.02, 0.5). The attack model is trained via full-parameter fine-tuning with a learning rate of 2e-5. Reconstruction quality is evaluated using standard generation metrics BLEU-4 and ROUGE-2. FedBert reports a Rouge-2 of 7.053 and a Bleu-4 of 28.57. Experiment results of FL-LLaMA is shown in Tab. \ref{tab:Attack_performance_modelA_noise}. From the results, we can see that when the client holds only the embedding layer (similar to FedBERT), the attack model achieves relatively high BLEU and ROUGE scores. However, under the FL-LLaMA framework, where the client also retains a portion of the LLMs block in addition to the embedding layer, all attack metrics approach zero. Tab. \ref{tab:performance_modelA_noise2} reports the downstream performance of $client_0$ after multi-client training, showing that secret attack allows training to proceed as usual without disrupting the federated process. These findings demonstrate that FL-LLaMA effectively mitigates model inversion attacks, thereby preserving the privacy of client-side data.

