Fig.~\ref{fig:w_wo_mask} shows a comparison of the time consumption between transferring the attention mask and transferring only the actual input length during distributed training. The bar chart represents the average time spent per token in FL-LLaMA's forward propagation, while the line chart represents the number of tokens generated per second by FL-LLaMA. It can be observed from the figure that transferring the compressed effective information of the attention mask plays a significant role in reducing the latency of distributed LLMs, with an average time reduction of 87.6\%.


\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.6]{images/kvcache.pdf}
    \caption{FL-LLaMA: Inference Speed Comparison(w/ vs. w/o KV Cache).}
    \label{fig:kvcache}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:kvcache} illustrates the effect of the KV cache technique on improving inference speed for different query lengths in distributed inference. The experimental results indicate that without the KV cache, the inference speed of FL-LLaMA significantly decreases as the query length increases, primarily due to the continuously increasing number of K and V vectors computed at each step in the self-attention mechanism. In contrast, with the KV cache technique, the client only needs to send the intermediate vector corresponding to a single token to the server, while reusing the previously generated K and V vectors, thereby effectively avoiding redundant computations. When the query length reaches 4000, the inference speed of FL-LLaMA with KV cache is 8.2 times that without it, and as the query length increases further, this advantage will continue to expand, greatly alleviating the computational burden and operational latency of distributed LLMs. 
